window.EXPL = {"2014A-p10#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> rewrite the LP with equality constraints so simplex can run on it.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> turn each inequality into an equation by adding one non-negative variable that absorbs the gap between the two sides.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" leaves room below the bound, so we <em>add</em> slack; a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" overshoots the bound, so we <em>subtract</em> surplus.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> simplex only knows how to walk between vertices of a system of equalities in non-negative variables, so the first thing you always do is manufacture that form.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> constraint.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> may fall short of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, so add slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> exactly when the original held.</li>\n  <li><b>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> constraint.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></math> may exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow></math>, so subtract surplus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Leave the objective alone.</b> The new variables get coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is unchanged, and every variable stays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Putting it together:</p>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mrow><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div>\n<p>Two equations in five non-negative variables — the shape simplex wants, with a basis (here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>) and the rest set to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> read off the vertex (point) that goes with the basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> set the three non-basic variables to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, then solve the two equations for the two basic variables.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a basis names which variables are \"on\"; everything else is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and the two equalities pin down the rest.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> every basic feasible solution is just \"non-basic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, solve for the basics,\" so this is the mechanical way to turn a basis into an actual point.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Zero out the non-basics.</b> The basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</b> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</b> From the second equation, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></math>; substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the vertex is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Both basic values are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so it is a genuine feasible corner — and these two rewritten equations are the \"dictionary\" simplex reads to pivot in the next parts.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> find which non-basic variable enters the basis on the first pivot.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> rewrite the objective using only the non-basic variables and pick the one whose coefficient is most positive.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a positive coefficient means raising that variable raises the objective, so the biggest coefficient is the steepest climb.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> at a vertex the basic variables are locked to the non-basics; writing the objective in the free variables shows exactly which direction pays off.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</b> Using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the objective becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Compare coefficients.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> improve, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>'s rate is larger.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> enters the basis — the most positive coefficient, i.e. the best gain per unit. This is the standard Dantzig rule, and it sets up the ratio test in part (d).</p>\n", "2014A-p10#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> find which basic variable leaves as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> enters.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (others still <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) and see which basic variable hits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> first.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the entering variable can only grow until some basic variable would go negative; that variable is the one that leaves.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> feasibility needs every variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the min-ratio test just asks \"which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> wall do we hit first?\"</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Write basics in terms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> sets no limit.</b> It has no <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> term, so it stays at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> no matter how large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> grows.</li>\n  <li><b><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> sets the limit.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> (ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves the basis and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> enters at value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>; the new basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#e": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> compute the objective value at the vertex reached after the first pivot.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> plug the new variable values into the non-basic form of the objective from part (c).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> that form already bakes in the constant, so evaluating it is just substitution.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the non-basic form is the cleanest place to read the objective, since the leftover non-basic variables are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Take the objective.</b> From part (c) it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>New values after the pivot.</b> Basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Substitute.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>32</mn></mrow></math> — which matches the intuition: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> climbed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> units at rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></math> to the starting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2014A-p4#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write the minimum-weight dominating set problem as an ILP.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> one binary variable per vertex for membership, a cost objective, and one constraint per vertex saying it is dominated.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is dominated exactly when at least one vertex of its closed neighborhood <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is chosen.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"every vertex must have someone chosen nearby\" is a covering condition, which turns directly into a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> constraint per vertex.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Variables.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector is the same as a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Objective.</b> The weight of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> (unchosen vertices drop out), and we minimize it.</li>\n  <li><b>Domination constraint.</b> For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> values this holds exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> itself or one of its neighbors is chosen.</li>\n  <li><b>The ILP.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector is a dominating set of that weight and vice versa, so the ILP optimum is the minimum weight dominating set.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014A-p4#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> give an instance where the LP relaxation's optimum is strictly below the integer optimum.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> use the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>-cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> with all weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; set three vertices to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> and one to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> fractional values let domination be \"shared\" across a closed neighborhood, meeting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> more cheaply than any integer choice can.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> a short even cycle is the classic small gap example — each closed neighborhood overlaps its neighbors, so half-values pile up to exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Setup.</b> On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math>, each vertex has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> neighbors, so its closed neighborhood has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices. Set one vertex to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and the other three to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Feasibility.</b> The zero vertex sees its two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> neighbors: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> vertex has at least two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> vertices in its closed neighborhood, so its sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All constraints hold.</li>\n  <li><b>LP cost.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so the LP optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Integer cost.</b> One vertex dominates only itself and its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> neighbors — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> vertices, missing the opposite corner — so a dominating set needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> vertices, cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Hence LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> ILP: not integral.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014A-p5#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Give a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for the minimum-cost subgraph in which every receiver <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> has a path to <em>some</em> sender in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> (senders may differ across receivers).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Merge all senders into one artificial terminal so the task becomes an ordinary Steiner tree, then apply the known <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for Steiner tree.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A zero-cost hub attached to every sender makes \"reach some sender\" the same as \"reach the hub\", collapsing many targets into a single required terminal.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Connect each receiver to any one of a group\" is exactly a Steiner-tree shape once the group is contracted to a point, and we already have a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation there — so reduce rather than build from scratch.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Reduction.</b> Add one new vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> and connect it to every sender <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with a cost-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> edge; call this graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Why it matches.</b> In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, a receiver reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> iff it reaches some sender: any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>-to-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> path must use a zero-cost edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so it passes through a sender, and conversely any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>-to-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> path extends to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> for free.</li>\n<li><b>Steiner formulation.</b> A minimum-cost subgraph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> connecting all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is a minimum Steiner tree with terminals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; since the added edges cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, its optimal cost equals the optimum of the original problem.</li>\n<li><b>Apply and finish.</b> Run the known <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for Steiner tree on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> with terminals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. It returns cost at most twice optimal; dropping the free artificial edges gives a subgraph of the original graph that is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014A-p6#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-bit integers can be multiplied in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time (Karatsuba).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> split each number into high/low halves, expand the product, and compute it with only three half-size multiplications instead of four.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the middle term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> is recovered from the single product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by subtracting off <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, which we compute anyway.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the naive expansion needs four half-products, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; killing one multiplication drops the branching factor to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and beats <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Split.</b> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-bit numbers (high and low halves; the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is a shift).</li>\n  <li><b>Expand.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. Naively this is the four products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>The trick.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. So we only need three half-size products: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Cost of the glue.</b> The remaining additions and shifts (by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>) are each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by the allowed basic operations, so the non-recursive work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Solve.</b> Master Theorem with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1.585</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Intuitively, saving one of every four multiplications compounds across all the recursion levels into a genuinely smaller exponent.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014A-p8#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Give a randomized MAX-3SAT algorithm that is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>-approximation in expectation, and prove it.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Assign each variable a fair coin, compute the per-clause success probability, and sum with linearity of expectation.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A random assignment satisfies each clause with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, and expectations of the per-clause indicators add up regardless of dependence.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Linearity of expectation lets us total per-clause probabilities without worrying about clauses sharing variables — no independence required.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Algorithm.</b> Set each variable to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> independently and uniformly at random.</li>\n<li><b>One clause.</b> A 3-literal clause fails only if all three literals are false. The literals are from distinct variables, so independent, each false with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>unsatisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>satisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Indicators.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is satisfied, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> count the satisfied clauses.</li>\n<li><b>Linearity.</b> Even though clauses may share variables, <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Ratio.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, so the algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>-approximation in expectation.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014A-p8#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Bound, in closed form, the probability the random assignment satisfies fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> of the clauses.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Flip to counting <em>unsatisfied</em> clauses (a nonnegative quantity), then apply Markov's inequality.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> \"Few satisfied\" is the same event as \"many unsatisfied,\" and the unsatisfied count has small expectation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, which Markov turns into a tail bound.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The clauses can share variables, so the satisfied indicators aren't independent; Markov needs only the mean, so it's the safe tool.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Rewrite the event.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> the number of satisfied clauses, \"fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> satisfied\" is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, equivalently <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for the unsatisfied count.</li>\n<li><b>Its expectation.</b> From part (a), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so by linearity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Markov.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Why not Chernoff.</b> Chernoff would need the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> independent, but shared variables make them dependent. If one wrongly assumed independence it would give a much smaller bound like <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>168</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> — sharper but not justified here, so the honest closed-form answer is the Markov <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014A-p9#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> Any maximal matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for maximum matching: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is a maximum matching.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Show every edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is blocked by a vertex that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> already uses, then count those vertices.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Maximality means no edge can be added, so every optimal edge must share an endpoint with some edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Maximal but maybe not maximum\" invites comparing the two matchings vertex by vertex, and maximality is precisely a statement about which vertices are already occupied.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>-edge is blocked.</b> Take any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. At least one endpoint of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> is used by some edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>; otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> would still be a matching, contradicting maximality.</li>\n  <li><b>Charge each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>-edge to a used vertex.</b> Map <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> to one of its endpoints lying in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the set of vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> touches, and note <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>The map is injective.</b> Edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> are vertex-disjoint, so distinct <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>-edges map to distinct vertices.</li>\n  <li><b>Count.</b> The injection gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> also cannot exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>, any maximal matching sits within a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> of the best one, which is the claimed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n", "2014B-p7#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write bin packing as an integer linear program and explain the variables.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Use 0/1 variables for \"item in bin\" and \"bin open,\" minimize open bins, and add constraints for coverage, linkage, and capacity.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Two families of indicators — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> placing items and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> opening bins — let the objective count exactly the bins actually used.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Packing is a set of yes/no placements, so binary variables model it directly; the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> link is what ties usage to the count we minimize.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Bound the bins.</b> There's always a packing into at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> bins (one item per bin), so index candidate bins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Variables.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> goes in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is open (used at all). Both are in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Objective.</b> Minimize the number of open bins, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Each item packed.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> (at optimum exactly one bin).</li>\n<li><b>Only open bins used.</b> If item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> must be open: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. This links the two variable families so the objective really counts every bin we use.</li>\n<li><b>Capacity, then assemble.</b> Each bin holds weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Altogether <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;s.t.&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n", "2014B-p7#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> With the number of bins fixed, give a 2-approximation for the smallest common bin capacity that fits all items.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Recognize this as minimizing the maximum load over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> bins, then run the greedy least-loaded scheduler and report its max load.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The minimum feasible capacity is exactly the minimum achievable maximum bin load — so a good makespan algorithm solves it.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Minimize the largest bin\" is the makespan problem already solved in class, whose greedy list-scheduler is a known 2-approximation.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Reformulate.</b> With capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, all items fit iff we can assign them so every bin's load is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. The least such <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>max&#x000A0;bin&#x000A0;load</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so minimizing capacity equals minimizing makespan.</li>\n<li><b>Algorithm.</b> Process items one by one, placing each into the currently least-loaded bin; output the resulting maximum bin load as the capacity.</li>\n<li><b>Why it's within 2.</b> This is the pset-2 scheduling result. Two lower bounds on the optimal capacity: it's at least the average load <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and at least the largest item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. When the busiest bin got its last item, that bin was least-loaded, so its load-before was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> average <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>; one more item (size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>) keeps it <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Conclusion.</b> The reported capacity is at most twice the minimum feasible capacity, a 2-approximation running in polynomial time.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014B-p8#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> The integer program IP1 is the minimum vertex cover problem.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Read off what each piece of the program — variables, constraints, objective — means on the graph.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> picks a vertex set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and the edge constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> says every edge has an endpoint in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> — a vertex cover.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Naming an ILP is done by translating its ingredients into graph language, so we interpret each line directly.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Variables = chosen set.</b> Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> marks whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is selected, so a solution is a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Constraints = covering.</b> For edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> holds iff at least one endpoint is chosen — exactly \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> covers every edge.\"</li>\n<li><b>Objective = smallest set.</b> Minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> minimizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Covering every edge with as few vertices as possible is the minimum vertex cover of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2014B-p8#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write LP1, the relaxation of the vertex-cover ILP IP1.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Drop integrality: replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and note the upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is unnecessary.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A relaxation enlarges the feasible region from the box <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and for this min-problem the cap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> never binds.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Relaxing an ILP always means loosening the integrality constraint into the tightest continuous version, so we just swap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>The relaxed program.</b> <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Why no <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</b> The objective minimizes and all constraints on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> are lower bounds. If some optimum had <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, lowering it to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> still satisfies every edge (an endpoint of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> already makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) and decreases the objective.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So no optimal solution ever needs a coordinate above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; that's why <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is redundant.</p>\n", "2014B-p8#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write DLP1, the dual of the vertex-cover LP relaxation LP1.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Apply the mechanical dual rules: one dual variable per primal constraint, one dual constraint per primal variable.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">\n<span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Each edge constraint becomes a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> becomes the constraint that its incident edge-weights sum to at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Dualizing is a fixed recipe once the primal is in standard form, so we turn the crank rather than invent anything.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Dual variables.</b> One <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> per edge constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Dual objective.</b> The primal right-hand sides are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the dual maximizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Dual constraints.</b> Each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> (objective coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) appears in the constraints of edges touching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So DLP1 is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: put weights on edges so each vertex's total is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, a fractional matching.</p>\n", "2014B-p8#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> The integer version of the dual DLP1 is the maximum matching problem.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Add integrality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and read off what the picked edge set must satisfy.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The per-vertex constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> says at most one chosen edge touches each vertex — that is exactly a matching.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Naming an ILP is translating its ingredients to graph language, so we interpret the constraints on the chosen edges.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Variables = chosen edges.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> marks whether edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is picked, so a solution is a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Constraints = matching.</b> For each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, at most one incident edge is picked, i.e. no two chosen edges share an endpoint.</li>\n<li><b>Objective = most edges.</b> Maximizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> maximizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The largest set of pairwise non-adjacent edges is the maximum matching — the natural counterpart to the minimum vertex cover of part (a).</p>\n", "2015A-p5#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> find a closed-form number of samples so the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>-estimate is within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>&#x00025;</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> sample points in the unit cube, count how many land in the radius-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> ball, turn the accuracy target into a deviation bound, and force Chernoff below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the fraction of points inside the ball estimates its volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, and Chernoff says the relative error stays small once you take enough samples.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the estimator is a sum of independent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indicators, so a Chernoff bound on its relative deviation is the direct tool.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Set up the estimator.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th sample landing in the ball; the cube has volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the mean is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, and the algorithm outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, whose expectation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Translate accuracy into a deviation.</b> We want <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Dividing by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> and multiplying by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, this is a deviation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>; so the relative error is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Apply two-sided Chernoff.</b> For a sum of independent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variables, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>: <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1800</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Force it below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> and solve.</b> Require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1800</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1800</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; taking logs gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1800</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, so <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1800</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> Any such <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> works &mdash; a closed form, no numeric evaluation needed.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015A-p6#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Prove that after packing all items of weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> bins are not full.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Induct on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>; at each new weight level the items divide <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> evenly, so a fresh bin isn't opened until the current one is exactly full.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Because every weight is a unit fraction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, items of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> can only leave one partly-filled bin behind — the last one opened.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A bound that grows by one as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> increases by one is the classic shape for induction on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Base case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</b> Items of weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> are the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>'s (each fills a bin) and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>'s (two fill a bin). The only non-full bin can be one holding a single unpaired <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Inductive hypothesis.</b> After packing all items of weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, assume at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> non-full bins.</li>\n<li><b>Add the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> items.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the algorithm keeps dropping <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> items into a bin until it hits exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, never opening a new bin while the current one has room. So every bin opened in this phase, except possibly the last, ends up completely full.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">That's the old <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> non-full bins plus at most one new one, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The induction closes.</p>\n", "2015A-p6#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> If the last item to open a new bin had weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, lower-bound the load of every bin except that last one by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Use why a new bin gets opened at all: the item didn't fit anywhere else.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> item is opened into a new bin only when every existing bin has less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> free — meaning each was already more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> full.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The algorithm opens a bin only as a last resort, so the opening event itself certifies that all other bins are nearly full.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Read the opening event.</b> When this <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> item was placed in a new bin, it fit in no existing bin, so every existing bin had free space below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Turn free space into load.</b> Free space under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> means each such bin was filled to more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Loads never drop.</b> This was the last opening event, so later items only add to existing bins; no bin's load decreases afterward.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So in the final packing, every bin other than the last-opened holds items of total weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2015A-p6#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Prove the algorithm uses at most one bin more than the optimum (an additive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>-approximation).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Bound the total item weight from below using parts (a) and (b), then compare with how many bins OPT needs.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> If the total weight is more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, then even a perfect packing needs at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> bins, while the algorithm uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Total weight is the universal lower bound on bins (each holds at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), so counting weight is how you compare against OPT.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Bound the weight.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> be the full bins and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> the non-full bins; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> opened the last bin. Full bins give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>; by part (b) each of the other <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> non-full bins gives at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Total weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Use part (a).</b> Part (a) gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Hence total weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Compare with OPT.</b> Every bin holds at most weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>total&#x000A0;weight</mtext><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Being an integer, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The algorithm uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> bins and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so it uses at most one extra bin — an additive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n", "2015A-p7#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Add slacks and perform the first simplex pivot, writing the objective and basic variables in terms of the non-basic ones.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Build the starting dictionary with slacks, pick an entering variable with positive objective coefficient, do the ratio test, and substitute.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> One pivot swaps one non-basic variable for one basic variable and rewrites every row in terms of the new non-basic set.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Slack variables turn \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" rows into equalities you can read as a dictionary, and the simplex pivot is the mechanical way to raise the objective.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Initial dictionary.</b> Add slacks <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; the non-basic variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> start at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Entering variable.</b> Pick a non-basic variable with a positive objective coefficient; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> has the largest (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> enters.</li>\n<li><b>Ratio test.</b> Raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and see which basic hits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> first: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> allows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> only grows (coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> allows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. The tightest is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves.</li>\n<li><b>Solve for it.</b> From the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> row, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Substitute everywhere.</b> Plug this into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>37</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>11</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">(The printed solution writes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> row, a small slip — substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> into the original <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> adds another <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math>.)</p>\n", "2015A-p7#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write the dual of the maximization LP from part (a).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Apply the standard recipe: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Each primal constraint becomes a dual variable, and each primal variable becomes a dual constraint.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The problem is already in canonical <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> form, so the dual is just the mechanical transpose of that recipe.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Dual variables and objective.</b> Three constraints give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; their right-hand sides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> form the objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>12</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>One dual constraint per primal variable.</b> Read each column of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and set it <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> that variable's objective coefficient:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Assemble.</b> The dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>12</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> subject to those four \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015A-p7#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> The dual optimum is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Exhibit one feasible primal solution of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math> and invoke strong duality.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Any feasible primal value is a lower bound on the primal optimum, which equals the dual optimum.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> You never need to solve the dual — a single feasible primal point plus the duality theorem pins down the bound for free.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Reuse part (a).</b> After the pivot, set the non-basic variables to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Check feasibility and value.</b> All variables are non-negative, so every primal constraint holds; the objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Lower-bound the primal.</b> Since the primal is a maximization with a feasible point of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>, its optimum is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Cross to the dual.</b> By strong duality the dual optimum equals the primal optimum, so the dual optimum is also at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015A-p7#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Model <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> as a linear program.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Replace each absolute value by a fresh variable bounded below by the expression and its negation, then minimize the sum.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is the smallest number that is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Absolute value is not linear, but under a minimization it can be squeezed from above by a new variable that the objective automatically pushes down to the true value.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Name the pieces.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> stand for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>; the objective becomes the linear <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Sandwich each one.</b> Force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> above the expression and its negation:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Why it's exact.</b> These give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>; since we minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, each is driven down to equal its absolute value at the optimum.</li>\n<li><b>Finish.</b> Add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> stay free. The full LP is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> subject to the four constraints above.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015B-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is the min-weight perfect matching and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> the min TSP tour.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> split an optimal tour's edges into two perfect matchings; the cheaper one is at most half the tour, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is no larger.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a cycle on an even number of vertices decomposes into two disjoint perfect matchings by taking alternate edges.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> to upper-bound the best matching, the natural trick is to exhibit a cheap matching, and a tour hands you two of them for free.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Setup.</b> Assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> even (needed for a perfect matching). Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the vertices in the order of a minimum TSP tour, whose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Two matchings from the tour.</b> Take alternate edges: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Each touches every vertex once, so both are perfect matchings, and together they use every tour edge exactly once: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>One is cheap.</b> Since the two weights sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, the smaller satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> (the min is at most the average).</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is the minimum over all perfect matchings, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015B-p5#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Turn an always-correct Las Vegas algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> (expected time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>) into a Monte Carlo algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> running in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time and correct with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> as a black box but cut it off after a fixed step budget, returning a default answer if it hasn't finished.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> only \"fails\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> when it runs unusually long, and a long run is rare because its expected time is small.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> To bound the probability of a large running time from just its expectation, Markov's inequality is the natural tool, so you set the cutoff as a multiple of the expected time.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps. If it finishes, output its answer; otherwise halt it and output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Running time.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> never runs past <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, so it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> on every execution.</li>\n<li><b>When it errs.</b> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> finishes, its answer is correct (Las Vegas), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is correct. The only error is when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> doesn't finish in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> outputs the default <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Bound that chance.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, Markov gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is wrong with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. correct at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> of the time. The bound uses only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so it depends on the algorithm's randomness, not on the input.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> this ILP is the <em>maximum independent set</em> problem.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> read what each variable and constraint says in graph language, and match it to a known problem.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> is just a yes/no for \"is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> chosen\", so a solution is a vertex subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> whenever you see one binary variable per vertex plus a per-edge constraint, translate literally — the graph problem falls right out.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Objective.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> counts the chosen vertices, so we want a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> as large as possible.</li>\n  <li><b>Edge constraint.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> forbids <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: the two endpoints of an edge can't both be in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Name it.</b> \"no edge has both endpoints inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>\" is exactly the definition of an independent set, and we maximize its size — the maximum independent set.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015B-p7#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write the LP relaxation of the maximum-independent-set ILP.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> keep the objective and constraints, and just drop the integrality requirement.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> \"relaxation\" means we let each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> be any real number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> instead of only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, which makes the program solvable in polynomial time.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> replacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is the standard recipe for turning any ILP into its LP relaxation.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Copy the rest unchanged.</b> Objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> and edge constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> stay exactly as they were.</li>\n  <li><b>Swap integrality for nonnegativity.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. No explicit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is needed: for any edge, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> already forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is connected, so every vertex lies on an edge).</li>\n  <li><b>The LP.</b>\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n", "2015B-p7#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the LP optimum is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> exhibit one concrete feasible point whose value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>; the optimum can only be higher.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> setting every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> satisfies every edge constraint exactly, and the objective sums to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> to lower-bound a maximization LP you never need the true optimum — any feasible solution gives a valid bound, so reach for the simplest symmetric guess.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Propose a point.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Check feasibility.</b> Each edge gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Feasible.</li>\n  <li><b>Compute its value.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Conclude.</b> The optimum is the best over all feasible points, so it is at least this one's value, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. (Worth noting: the true integer answer can be as small as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, e.g. on a clique — so the relaxation can overshoot by a factor of about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.)</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015B-p7#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> describe a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>-vertex graph whose maximum independent set has size exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> take the clique <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> — every pair of vertices joined by an edge.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> if every pair is adjacent, no two vertices can sit together in an independent set, so an independent set can hold at most one vertex.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> we want independence forced to be tiny, and the densest possible graph — the clique — makes every pair an obstacle.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>The graph.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the complete graph on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></math> vertices.</li>\n  <li><b>At most one vertex.</b> Any two distinct <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> are adjacent, so any set with two vertices contains an edge and fails independence.</li>\n  <li><b>One is achievable.</b> A single vertex is trivially independent, so the maximum is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. (Together with part (c): here the LP optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></math> while the ILP optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — a large integrality gap.)</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015B-p7#e": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write the dual of the LP in (b).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> apply the mechanical duality rules to the primal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> one dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> per edge; the dual minimizes, primal right-hand sides become the dual objective, and primal objective coefficients become the dual right-hand sides.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the primal is in the clean \"max, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> constraints, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\" form, so its dual is \"min, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> constraints, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\" read straight off the rules.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Dual variables.</b> One <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> per primal constraint, i.e. per edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Dual objective.</b> Each edge constraint has right-hand side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Dual constraints.</b> One per primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> appears in exactly the edge constraints touching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, each with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and its objective coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; this gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>The dual.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> — the total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> on edges touching each vertex is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015B-p7#f": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> reading the dual of (e) as an ILP gives the <em>minimum edge cover</em> problem.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and translate each part into edge/vertex language.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is a yes/no for \"select edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\", so a solution is a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> of edges.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the dual constraint \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> around each vertex\" is the signature of a covering problem — read literally, it says every vertex must be touched.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Constraint.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> says at least one chosen edge is incident to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>: every vertex is covered by some selected edge.</li>\n  <li><b>Objective.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> counts selected edges, so we want as few as possible.</li>\n  <li><b>Name it.</b> Choosing a smallest edge set that touches every vertex is exactly the minimum edge cover — the natural companion to the maximum independent set we started from.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2015B-p7#g": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> a polynomial-time algorithm that finds a minimum edge cover, plus a short correctness argument.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> take a maximum matching, then add one cheap edge for each still-uncovered vertex; argue no cover can do better.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a matching covers two new vertices per edge — the most efficient covering possible — so building around a maximum matching minimizes the edge count.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"cover every vertex with few edges\" begs for matching: each matching edge kills two birds at once, and matching is a poly-time tool from class.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Algorithm.</b> Compute a maximum matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> (poly time, e.g. the matching algorithm from class). It has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> edges covering <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> distinct vertices. For each of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> still-uncovered vertices, add one arbitrary incident edge. (An edge cover exists since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is connected, so no vertex is isolated.)</li>\n  <li><b>Size of the output.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Lower bound on any cover.</b> Take any optimal cover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> and assign each vertex to one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>-edge covering it. Each edge is assigned at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> vertices, and the edges assigned two vertices are vertex-disjoint — a matching — so there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext></mrow></math> of them.</li>\n  <li><b>Conclude.</b> Counting shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> uses at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></math> edges, so no cover beats ours. Intuitively: you can't cover the vertices with fewer edges than \"one per vertex, minus a bonus for every pair a matching lets you share,\" and a maximum matching grabs every available bonus.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2016A-p5#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False — the greedy algorithm is not a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for maximum cut.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Trace the algorithm carefully and show it can put every vertex on one side, giving a cut of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Both sides start empty and the test is a strict \"more neighbors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>\", which no vertex can ever pass, so everything drifts to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> To refute an approximation claim, the cleanest move is one instance where the algorithm's output is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> but the optimum is positive, making the ratio unbounded.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Read the rule.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> both start empty. A vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> joins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> only if it has strictly more neighbors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> than in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>; otherwise it joins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> (ties go to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li><b>First vertex.</b> With both sets empty, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> neighbors in each; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is false, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> goes to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>, leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> empty.</li>\n<li><b>Everything follows to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>.</b> As long as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is empty, every vertex has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> neighbors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, so it never has strictly more there — it always joins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. Hence all vertices end in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>The cut is zero.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> empty, no edge crosses, so the cut value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, while any graph with an edge has optimum at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Since the algorithm outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> while the optimum is positive, no constant ratio (let alone <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>) can hold, so the claim is refuted.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write maximum-weight matching as a binary integer program.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> put one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variable on each edge saying whether it is picked, maximize the total weight of picked edges, and stop any vertex from being used twice.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a matching is exactly a set of edges where no vertex is used more than once, and \"used at most once\" is a linear inequality at each vertex.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the definition of a matching is a local rule per vertex (degree at most one), so it drops straight into one constraint per vertex — the natural way to turn it into an ILP.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Choose the variables.</b> For each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> meaning \"this edge is in the matching.\"</li>\n<li><b>Write the objective.</b> We want the heaviest matching: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Add the matching constraint.</b> At each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the picked edges touching it may total at most one: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Force integrality.</b> Require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> so each edge is fully in or fully out.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector covers each vertex at most once, so it is exactly a matching, and its objective value is that matching's weight.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write the dual of the LP relaxation from part (a): <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for every edge, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> apply the standard primal-dual recipe: one dual variable per primal constraint (vertex), one dual constraint per primal variable (edge), and flip max to min.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> each edge variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> appears in exactly its two endpoint constraints, so its dual constraint involves only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the primal has \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which is the textbook shape whose dual is a minimization with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints — just read the recipe off the columns.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Relax first.</b> Drop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> down to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; the LP is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Dual variables.</b> One per vertex constraint: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Dual constraints.</b> One per edge. Edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> sits in the constraints of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the objective coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Dual objective.</b> The primal right-hand sides are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so we minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">By weak duality any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> upper-bounds the matching value, which is the covering picture spelled out in part (c).</p>\n", "2016A-p6#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the dual from part (b) is (fractional) <b>weighted vertex cover</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> read each variable as an amount of \"resource\" placed on a vertex and read each constraint as a demand on an edge.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> says every edge's demand <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is met by the resource sitting on its two endpoints.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"sum over the two endpoints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> something, minimize total\" is the signature of a covering problem, so naming it as vertex cover is immediate.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Variables as weights.</b> Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is how much weight (budget) we put on vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Constraints as coverage.</b> For every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the weights on its endpoints must total at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Objective as thrift.</b> Minimize the total weight placed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So it is: put enough weight on the vertices that each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> has at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> on its endpoints, using as little total weight as possible — fractional because we are in the LP, not the integer program.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> add slack variables and rewrite the LP in the equality (dictionary) form that simplex runs on.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> turn each inequality into an equality with a nonnegative slack/surplus variable, then solve each equation for its slack.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraint loses a surplus, a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraint gains a slack, and once everything is an equality simplex can read a starting vertex by zeroing the nonbasic variables.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> simplex only works on equalities plus nonnegativity, so converting the two inequalities is the required setup step.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Fix the \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" row.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> needs a surplus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> subtracted: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Fix the \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" row.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math> needs a slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> added: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>State the equality form.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Solve for the slacks.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the dictionary form.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Now the basic variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> are written in terms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so setting those to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> hands simplex a candidate vertex to start from.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#e": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> gives the vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> a basis names which variables may be nonzero; set the others to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and solve the two equality constraints for the basic ones.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the nonbasic variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which collapses each constraint to a single unknown.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> a vertex of the feasible region is exactly a basic solution, so \"read off the vertex\" just means zero the nonbasic variables and back out the rest.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Identify nonbasic.</b> Of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</b> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math> with the zeros, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</b> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Both basic values came out nonnegative (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></math>), so the vertex is feasible — the basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> sits at a real corner of the region.</p>\n", "2017A-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the running time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> collect all the costs into one recurrence, then read the answer off the master theorem.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> subproblems of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> shrink faster than the linear work per call, so the top-level <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> dominates.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> a recurrence of the shape <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is exactly what the master theorem is built to solve, so we just line up the pieces.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Write the recurrence.</b> There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> calls on size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. The non-recursive work is the divide step <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> plus the combine step <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> dominates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, that total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Compare the two costs.</b> The recursion-tree cost is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02248;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>0.79</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, while the top-level work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, the linear term is polynomially larger.</li>\n<li><b>Read off the answer.</b> The work is dominated by the top of the tree, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The base case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow></math> solved in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>500</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> only affects bounded inputs and does not change the asymptotics. Intuitively, all the recursive pieces together cost less than the single linear pass at the top.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017A-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the running time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> unroll the recurrence one level at a time and sum the per-level costs, which shrink geometrically.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> each level halves both dimensions, so its work is half of the level above; a geometric series in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> sums to at most twice the top level.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> there is only a single chain of recursive calls (no branching), so the total is just the sum down that chain, and a halving series is the classic thing to add up.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Cost per level.</b> Write the per-call work <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The top level costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; the recursive call is on a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x0230B;</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x0230B;</mi></mrow></math> matrix, costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; the next is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and so on.</li>\n<li><b>Sum the levels.</b> <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Bound the geometric series.</b> The bracket has ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so it is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Match with a lower bound.</b> The very first level already does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> work, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Together this pins it to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> &mdash; the top level sets the size and everything below adds only a constant factor.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017A-p1#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> <b>True</b> &mdash; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> always holds.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> apply linearity of expectation, and note it needs no independence.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> expectation is a weighted sum over outcomes, and sums split apart regardless of how the variables relate.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the statement is a linear combination of two random variables, which is the exact form linearity of expectation covers.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>State the rule.</b> For any random variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> on the same sample space and constants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> this is precisely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Note independence is irrelevant.</b> Linearity comes straight from the definition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a sum always splits term by term. Independence is only needed for products like <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, never for a linear combination &mdash; so the identity holds even for dependent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017A-p1#e": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> <b>True</b> &mdash; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is a PTAS for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> check the two things a PTAS asks for: a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> guarantee, and running time polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for each fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a PTAS is allowed to let the exponent grow as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> shrinks &mdash; it only needs to be polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> is a fixed constant.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the running time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> sitting in the exponent, which is the tell-tale sign to check the PTAS definition rather than the stricter FPTAS one.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Quality is fine.</b> By assumption, for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the output has cost at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times optimal &mdash; exactly the approximation a PTAS requires.</li>\n<li><b>Runtime is fine for fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> as a constant; then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> is a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The definition permits this, since it only demands polynomiality in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> per fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Wrap up.</b> Both conditions hold, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is a PTAS. (It is not an FPTAS, which would also need polynomial dependence on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> &mdash; but that was not asked.)</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017A-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — the described Christofides variant is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for metric Path-TSP.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Show the Euler path exists, bound the tree cost by OPT, bound the matching by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> using two candidate matchings, then add and shortcut.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The odd vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, in the order OPT visits them, split OPT's edges into subpaths that two alternating matchings cover once each — so the cheaper matching costs at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Charging a matching against OPT by walking the special vertices in OPT-order is the heart of the Christofides analysis; leaving two vertices unmatched is what adapts it from a tour to a path.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Euler path exists.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is nonempty (every leaf of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> is odd). Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> flips the parity of every matched vertex, so in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> exactly the two unmatched vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> stay odd; a connected graph with exactly two odd vertices has an Euler path.</li>\n  <li><b>Tree cost.</b> OPT is a Hamilton path, hence a spanning tree, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> is a minimum spanning tree, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Two candidate matchings.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is even; list its vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in OPT-order. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo></mrow></math>; each leaves exactly two vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> unmatched, so each is a valid candidate for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Charge to OPT.</b> OPT contains the disjoint subpaths <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo></mrow></math>; by the triangle inequality each matching edge costs at most its subpath, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> together use each subpath once, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Take the cheaper.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> is minimum, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, and shortcutting repeats only saves cost, giving a Hamilton path within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math> times optimal.</p>\n", "2017A-p4#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Find how many samples estimate the fraction of ones within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Sample bits, output the sample mean, and use Chernoff to size the number of samples <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> so the error stays under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math> with the required confidence.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Chernoff bounds the chance the sample mean strays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math> from the truth, and since the true fraction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> we can bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> without knowing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"How many samples for accuracy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> with confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>\" is exactly what a Chernoff concentration bound answers — set the tail probability to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> and solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Setup.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th sampled bit being a one; the samples are independent with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>, the true fraction. The estimate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Chernoff.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, where the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> covers both tails.</li>\n  <li><b>Impose confidence.</b> Require this <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>. Taking logs gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>30000</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>30000</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Drop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> always, taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>30000</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn></mrow></math> works for every possible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>30000</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>90000</mn></mrow></math> samples — a fixed constant, independent of the stream length — guarantee the estimate is within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2017A-p6#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write minimum hitting set as a binary integer linear program.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> put one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variable on each element, turn \"hit every set\" into linear constraints, and minimize the count of chosen elements.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> meets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>\" means at least one element of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is picked, which is just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> a yes/no choice per element is the natural way to model a subset, and indicator variables convert set conditions into linear inequalities.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Variables.</b> For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is in the hitting set. The chosen set is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Constraints.</b> The requirement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> says at least one element of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is picked. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> counts the picked elements of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, this becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Objective.</b> Minimize the size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Altogether: <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> values force an all-or-nothing choice, so optimal solutions are exactly minimum hitting sets.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017A-p6#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write the dual of the LP relaxation of the hitting-set program.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> relax to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, drop the useless upper bounds, then form the dual with one variable per set.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a min-LP with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> dualizes to a max-LP with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, swapping the roles of objective and right-hand side.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the mechanical dual recipe is the reliable way to get the dual, and clearing the redundant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> bounds first keeps it clean.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Relax and prune.</b> The relaxation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Since we minimize and every constraint has nonnegative coefficients, no optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> ever needs to exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> are redundant and drop out, leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>One dual variable per constraint.</b> Introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for each set-constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. The dual is a maximization with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints.</li>\n<li><b>Dual objective.</b> The primal right-hand sides are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the dual maximizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Dual constraints.</b> One per primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>: its objective coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and it appears in constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. So the dual is <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n", "2017A-p6#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the BIP version of the dual is the <b>set packing</b> problem: pick the largest collection of pairwise disjoint subsets from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> impose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> on the dual and read what each piece means combinatorially.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the constraint \"each element sits in at most one chosen set\" is exactly the statement that the chosen sets don't overlap.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> turning a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variable back into \"chosen / not chosen\" and a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" constraint into \"no sharing\" is the standard way to name what an ILP is really asking.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Interpret the variables.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means \"subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is chosen\"; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> be the chosen collection.</li>\n<li><b>Interpret the objective.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>D</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> counts the chosen subsets, and we maximize it.</li>\n<li><b>Interpret the constraint.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> says each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> lies in at most one chosen subset &mdash; so no two chosen subsets share an element. That is: find a maximum-size family of mutually disjoint subsets of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, the set packing problem.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017A-p6#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> rewrite the given LP in standard form: a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></math> with all \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints and all variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> fix each violation in turn &mdash; flip the nonpositive variables, split the equality into two inequalities, and orient every constraint as \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\".</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a variable constrained <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> by substituting its negative, and an equality is just two opposite inequalities.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> standard form has a fixed checklist of requirements, so you simply repair whatever fails one item at a time.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Flip the nonpositive variables.</b> Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> (so the new <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> (new <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>); <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> already. Now all variables are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Rewrite the objective.</b> Under those flips, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Split the equality.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Applying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>: the first is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>; negate the second to get \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\": <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Convert the last inequality.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Final standard form: <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n", "2017B-p2#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> The ratio-sorted greedy knapsack has unbounded approximation ratio.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Build one tiny two-item family where greedy grabs a worthless dense item and then can't fit the valuable one; let the gap grow with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Sorting by density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> can make greedy commit to a high-density crumb that blocks a much more valuable heavy item.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> To disprove any constant ratio, one explicit counterexample whose ratio scales with a free parameter is enough.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>The instance.</b> Capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>, two items: item 1 with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; item 2 with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Densities.</b> Item 1 has ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, item 2 has ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, greedy sorts item 1 first.</li>\n<li><b>What greedy does.</b> It takes item 1 (weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>), leaving capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so item 2 no longer fits. Greedy's value is just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>What OPT does.</b> Taking item 2 alone uses weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> and yields value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>The ratio grows.</b> On this instance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> can be arbitrarily large, no constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C1;</mi></mrow></math> survives (just pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C1;</mi></mrow></math>), so the ratio is unbounded.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017B-p2#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write knapsack as a binary integer linear program.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Give each item a 0/1 \"take it or not\" variable, then express total value and total weight as linear sums of those variables.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A 0/1 vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is exactly a subset of items, so maximizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> is the problem itself.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Choose a subset\" is naturally a yes/no decision per item, which is precisely what binary variables encode.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Variables.</b> One variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> per item, forced to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is chosen, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is left out.</li>\n<li><b>Objective.</b> The total value of the chosen set is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which we maximize.</li>\n<li><b>Capacity.</b> The total weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> must not exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Assemble.</b> <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> The integrality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is what forbids taking a fraction of an item, keeping the model faithful.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017B-p2#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write the LP relaxation of the knapsack BIP from part (b).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Keep the objective and capacity row, and just drop integrality — replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the integer endpoints of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>; allowing everything in between makes the program linear and polynomial-time solvable.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Relaxing integrality is the standard way to get a tractable LP whose optimum upper-bounds the hard integer optimum.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Relax the integrality.</b> Swap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for the two linear inequalities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, letting each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be any real in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Carry the rest over.</b> The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> are already linear, so they stay as is: <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Note what it buys.</b> This LP solves in polynomial time and its optimum is at least the integer optimum (every 0/1 solution is still feasible here). Reading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> as \"the fraction of item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> taken\" makes it the fractional knapsack.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017B-p2#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write the LP dual of the knapsack relaxation from part (c).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Apply the mechanical dual recipe: each primal constraint becomes a dual variable, each primal variable becomes a dual constraint, and max flips to min.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The one capacity row gives a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> give variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and the objective is built from the primal right-hand sides.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Dualizing is a fixed procedure — reading off constraint-vs-variable roles is all that's needed, no cleverness.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Assign dual variables.</b> The primal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> has the capacity row <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> (variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) and the bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). Both are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> since these are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> rows in a maximization.</li>\n<li><b>Build the dual objective.</b> Combine the primal right-hand sides: capacity RHS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> and each bound RHS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>One constraint per <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</b> Collect <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>'s coefficients across primal rows — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the capacity row and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in its own bound — and require their weighted sum to be at least the objective coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>: <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Write it out.</b> <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>W</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> direction comes from the primal variables being <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017B-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Show a random walk from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> reaches a target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, given cover time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Bound the expected time to hit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> by the cover time, then apply Markov at four times that mean.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Reaching a single vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is easier than covering all of them, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>hit&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; Markov converts that mean into a tail bound.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> We only have a bound on an expectation and want a \"within so many steps\" probability — Markov is exactly the mean-to-tail conversion.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Define the variable.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> be the number of steps until the walk first reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Bound its mean.</b> The cover time is the expected time to visit all vertices, which in particular visits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Apply Markov.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Complement.</b> Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is chosen precisely so Markov lands on the clean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> failure probability.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2017B-p4#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Prove some assignment always satisfies at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> clauses.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Pair any assignment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> with its bitwise negation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> and show every clause is caught by at least one of them.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A clause failed by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> has all its literals false, so flipping every variable makes them all true — the complement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> satisfies it.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> To force a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> guarantee, average two complementary assignments; if their satisfied counts sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, the larger clears <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>The pairing.</b> Fix any assignment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> flip every variable. Compare how many clauses each satisfies.</li>\n<li><b>Every clause is caught.</b> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> fails a clause, all three literals are false under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>; flipping every variable turns them all true, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> satisfies it. No clause is failed by both.</li>\n<li><b>Count.</b> Summing over clauses, (clauses satisfied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>) + (clauses satisfied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math>) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, since each clause contributes at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">If two nonnegative numbers add to at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, the bigger is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. So one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> clauses — such an assignment always exists.</p>\n", "2018A-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write and solve the recurrence for Strassen's matrix multiplication.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> read the recursive structure off the algorithm, then apply the Master Theorem.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Strassen makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> half-size multiplications (not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>); the extra additions are only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> work per level.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> a divide-and-conquer cost with equal-size subproblems is the exact shape the Master Theorem is built for.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Recurrence.</b> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> recursive products of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> matrices give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> matrix additions plus split/combine cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Master Theorem form.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, the leaf-heavy case, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Evaluate.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.807</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math> — below the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> of naive multiplication. Intuitively the many leaves at the bottom of the tree dominate the total.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2018A-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>T</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>T</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> this isn't Master-Theorem shaped (unequal subproblems), so reason level by level down the recursion tree.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the per-node work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (the split dominates the combine), and squaring shrinks the total work each level by a constant factor, so the top level dominates.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> when subproblem sizes differ, you can't just plug into a formula — but a geometric series across levels still settles it cleanly.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Local work.</b> The split costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the combine only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so a node of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Work shrinks per level.</b> The children's work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>36</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.70</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Each level is a constant factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> of the one above.</li>\n  <li><b>Sum it.</b> Total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a converging geometric series. The root alone already costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — the top level sets the pace.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2018A-p1#e": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the randomized min-cut algorithm (Karger's) is <em>Monte Carlo</em>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the distinction is which resource is fixed: Las Vegas always returns the correct answer with random runtime; Monte Carlo has bounded runtime but may be wrong.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> just check Karger's guarantee — its running time is fixed but its answer isn't always right, which is the Monte Carlo signature.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>How it runs.</b> Karger's algorithm contracts a uniformly random edge until two vertices remain, then outputs the surviving cut. It always halts after the same number of contractions — the runtime is fixed.</li>\n  <li><b>Why it can be wrong.</b> A single run returns a fixed minimum cut only with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, since it must never contract an edge of that cut. Fixed time, possibly wrong answer — that is Monte Carlo. (Repeating many times drives the error down, but each run is still Monte Carlo.)</li>\n</ol>\n", "2018A-p2#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Describe a polynomial 3-approximation for minimum hitting set when every subset has size at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Sweep the subsets in any order; whenever one is not yet hit, throw all of its (at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>) elements into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Grabbing an entire unhit subset both guarantees coverage and keeps the grabbed subsets disjoint, which is what caps the cost.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> This mirrors the maximal-matching idea behind the vertex-cover 2-approximation: hit greedily, and the \"size at most 3\" bound turns factor 2 into factor 3.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Algorithm.</b> Start with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>. Process the subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> in arbitrary order. When you reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, add all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02190;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>; otherwise do nothing.</li>\n  <li><b>It hits everything.</b> When we finish a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> it satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> — either it was already hit, or we just added all of it.</li>\n  <li><b>Cheap and disjoint.</b> Each add inserts at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> elements, and we add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> only when it currently shares nothing with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, so the added subsets are pairwise disjoint — the fact that drives the ratio in part (b).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So a simple one-pass greedy runs in polynomial time and returns a valid hitting set.</p>\n", "2018A-p2#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> greedy is a polynomial-time 3-approximation for minimum hitting set.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Show it runs in polynomial time, always outputs a hitting set, then sandwich its size between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> above and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> below.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The subsets we fully grab are disjoint, so no single element hits two of them — forcing any hitting set, including OPT, to spend at least one element per grabbed subset.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Disjointness is a built-in lower bound on OPT — the same trick as vertex cover's disjoint matched edges, adapted to sets of size at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Polynomial time.</b> Store <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> as a linked list; testing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mo>&#x022C3;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is bounded by the input size, so each of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> iterations is polynomial.</li>\n  <li><b>It is a hitting set.</b> After processing any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, so every subset is hit.</li>\n  <li><b>Upper bound.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of subsets that triggered an add. Each adds at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> elements, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Lower bound.</b> Those <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> added subsets are pairwise disjoint, so one element hits at most one of them; any hitting set needs at least one element per subset, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Combining, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> — the greedy stays within a factor of three of the best possible.</p>\n", "2018A-p2#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Give a 3-competitive online algorithm for hitting set when each subset has size at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Run the exact same greedy, treating the online arrival order as the \"arbitrary order\" the offline version already allows.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The offline greedy never uses knowledge of future subsets and never undoes a choice, so it works unchanged when subsets arrive one at a time.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> When an offline algorithm processes items in any order with irrevocable decisions, it is already an online algorithm — no redesign needed.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Algorithm.</b> Keep <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>. When a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> arrives, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> add all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>; otherwise leave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> unchanged.</li>\n  <li><b>Order doesn't matter.</b> Part (a)'s analysis allowed arbitrary processing order and never looked ahead, so let that order be the arrival order.</li>\n  <li><b>Requirements met.</b> After each arriving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> hits everything seen so far, and additions are never removed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The final <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is exactly what the offline greedy would build on that sequence, so its bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> carries over and the algorithm is 3-competitive.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write minimum-cost perfect matching as an integer linear program and say what the variables mean.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Put one binary variable on each edge for \"chosen or not,\" make the objective the total weight of chosen edges, and force every vertex to be covered exactly once.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A perfect matching is precisely a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> choice of edges where each vertex touches exactly one chosen edge.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Choose a subset of edges\" is the natural cue for one indicator variable per edge, and \"perfect matching\" becomes a linear count constraint at each vertex.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Variables.</b> For each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is in the matching and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is not.</li>\n  <li><b>Objective.</b> The cost of the chosen set is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which we minimize.</li>\n  <li><b>Perfect-matching constraint.</b> Each vertex must meet exactly one chosen edge: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. Equality (not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) is what forbids leaving a vertex uncovered.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The full program is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nEvery feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is exactly the indicator of a perfect matching, so an optimum is a minimum-cost one.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write the LP relaxation of the matching ILP and drop any redundant constraint.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, then notice the upper bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> are already forced by the vertex equalities.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A single edge value cannot exceed the sum at its endpoint, which equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> comes for free.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Relax and remove redundant constraints\" is the standard LP-relaxation exercise — swap integrality for an interval, then prune what other constraints already imply.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Relax integrality.</b> Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, keeping the vertex equalities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Upper bound is redundant.</b> For edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the endpoint equality at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and it contains the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Since every term is nonnegative, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> automatically.</li>\n  <li><b>Prune it.</b> So each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> follows from the vertex equality plus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and can be removed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The clean relaxation is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nOnly the vertex equalities and nonnegativity remain.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write the dual of the matching LP relaxation and state its variables.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Give one dual variable per vertex constraint, form the dual objective from the right-hand sides, and read off one dual constraint per primal edge variable.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Each edge variable appears in exactly its two endpoint constraints, so its dual constraint caps the sum of those two endpoint potentials by the edge weight.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Taking a dual is mechanical once you match dual variables to primal constraints and dual constraints to primal variables — the only judgment call is the sign rules.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Dual variables.</b> One per vertex constraint: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. Since each primal constraint is an <em>equality</em>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> is free (unconstrained in sign).</li>\n  <li><b>Objective.</b> The primal right-hand sides are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the dual maximizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Constraints, one per <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</b> Edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> appears with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in the constraints of its endpoints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, and has objective coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Primal min with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> gives a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" dual constraint: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The dual is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>free</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThink of each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> as a vertex potential; every edge caps the sum of its endpoints' potentials, and we maximize the total.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Rewrite <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> as a linear program.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Introduce an auxiliary variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> that upper-bounds the absolute value with two linear inequalities, then minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so minimizing that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> reproduces the absolute value.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> An absolute value in the objective is the textbook trigger for the \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> above both branches\" trick — it is the standard way to linearize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> inside a minimization.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Bracket the value.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> and impose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Together they say <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Setting the objective to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> drives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> down until one inequality is tight, forcing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. No sign constraint on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is needed — the two inequalities already give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Keep the feasibility constraint.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is already linear, so it stays as is.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The LP is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nEverything is linear in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, and the optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> under the constraint.</p>\n", "2018B-p2#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False — the min-degree greedy does not have approximation ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; on a family of graphs it returns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> while the optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> would mean greedy always finds a maximum independent set, so we only need one family of graphs where it does much worse, forever.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Picking the globally minimum-degree vertex can be a trap: that vertex drags in neighbors whose removal wipes out a large independent set that was sitting right there.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> To refute a claim you never argue in general — you build the single cleanest counterexample and just compute what greedy does versus what's possible.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>The graph.</b> For any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, with edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> (the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>'s form a clique).</li>\n  <li><b>Degrees.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>'s plus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>), and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>'s plus the other <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>'s). For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is the strict minimum-degree vertex.</li>\n  <li><b>What greedy does.</b> It grabs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> first and deletes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> with all its neighbors, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>'s. Only the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>-clique is left; it picks one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which deletes the whole clique. Now the graph is empty, so greedy stops with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>The optimum.</b> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>'s have no edges among themselves, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is an independent set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So greedy returns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> was available, and the ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> can be made as large as we like. In short, chasing the smallest-degree vertex once fooled greedy into throwing away the whole good set, so ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> simply can't hold.</p>\n", "2018B-p2#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — when every vertex has degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math>, the min-degree greedy runs in polynomial time and is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-approximation for maximum independent set.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Show the output is a valid independent set, then bound how many vertices each greedy pick can destroy, and compare the set's size to the trivial upper bound on the optimum.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Every iteration adds one vertex to the answer but deletes at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vertices from the graph, so the answer's size is at least a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> fraction of all vertices.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> With no exact formula for the optimum, the standard approximation move is to sandwich it: lower-bound what greedy gets and upper-bound what anyone could get, both against the same simple quantity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Output is independent.</b> Whenever a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, all of its neighbors are removed from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, so no later pick can be adjacent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> has no internal edges.</li>\n  <li><b>Trivial ceiling on the optimum.</b> An independent set is just a subset of vertices, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Cost of each pick.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of iterations. Each iteration adds exactly one vertex to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and removes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> together with its neighbors — at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math> vertices. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> and the total removed is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Counting bound.</b> Greedy stops only when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is empty, i.e. all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> vertices are gone, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Running time.</b> There are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> iterations; keeping vertex degrees in a heap lets us find the minimum-degree vertex and update after deletions in polynomial time.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So greedy always lands within a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> of the best possible. The intuition is simple: each vertex you take can only knock out a handful of others (its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math> neighbors plus itself), so you can never fall too far behind the optimum.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> Yes — the expected-runtime bound holds for every input, the worst-case array included.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Locate where the randomness actually comes from, then check what the guarantee is averaging over.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The only random thing is the pivot the algorithm flips for; the array itself is fixed, whatever an adversary hands us.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> For a randomized algorithm \"expected running time\" always means the average over its own coin flips, not over random inputs — so it can't be broken by a bad input.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Separate fixed from random.</b> The input array <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is arbitrary (possibly the nastiest one an adversary can pick); the pivots are chosen uniformly at random by the algorithm.</li>\n<li><b>Read off the conclusion.</b> The analysis bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> for a fixed but arbitrary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, averaging only over pivots. So the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> bound already covers the worst-case input — the answer is Yes.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2018B-p4#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> The expectation is over the algorithm's random pivot choices.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Ask what probability space <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> lives in, and name its only source of randomness.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The input is treated as fixed; the pivots are the coins, so averaging is over pivot sequences.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> This is the direct follow-up to part (a): whenever we write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> we must say what is random, and here it is only the pivots.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Input contributes nothing.</b> The array is fixed and arbitrary; it carries no randomness.</li>\n<li><b>Pivots are the coins.</b> At each recursive call the algorithm picks a pivot uniformly at random, and those choices decide which side we recurse on, hence how much work happens.</li>\n<li><b>State the answer.</b> So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a function of the pivot sequence, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> averages <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over all equally likely pivot sequences — the expectation is over the pivot choices.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2018B-p4#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Prove <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Condition on the pivot's rank, pessimistically charge each case to its larger side, then average over the uniform pivot.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Selection recurses on only one side, but bounding by the bigger side and counting how often each big size occurs collapses the sum to the upper half with a factor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> We don't know which side the recursion takes, so the safe upper bound is \"the worse of the two,\" and conditioning on the pivot is the clean way to enumerate the cases.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Condition on the pivot.</b> The pivot is uniform, so it is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th largest with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Partitioning always costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Charge the larger side.</b> If the pivot is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th largest, the two parts have sizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>; since we recurse on one but can't say which, upper-bound it by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Average over the pivot.</b> By total expectation, <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>max</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Collapse the max.</b> As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> runs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the two sizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> each sweep <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and the larger of the pair is always some index in the top half <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Each such index is hit by at most two values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, so the sum of maxima is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Finish.</b> Substituting gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, exactly the recurrence (rounding of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> ignored as allowed).</li>\n</ol>\n", "2018B-p4#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Use part (c)'s recurrence to prove by induction that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Guess <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, plug the guess into the recurrence, sum the arithmetic series, and pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> so the algebra closes.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The recurred sum only touches the top half of sizes, so its average is about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; leaving room <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> to absorb the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> partition cost.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A linear target with a recurrence over smaller sizes is the textbook setup for induction with an unknown constant chosen at the end.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Guess and hypothesis.</b> Claim <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> fixed later; assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> be the constant with partition cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Base case.</b> Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. Choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> holds.</li>\n<li><b>Apply recurrence + hypothesis.</b> <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Sum the series.</b> Using (first + last) times half the count: first term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, last <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> terms, so <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Simplify.</b> Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></li>\n<li><b>Close it.</b> We need <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; both requirements hold, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. In words: each level's work is a shrinking fraction of the last, so the total stays linear.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2018B-p4#e": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Turn the expected-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> selection algorithm into one that always runs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>25</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations and is correct with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Run capped attempts: abort any run once it passes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, retry up to 5 times with fresh randomness, and return the first run that finishes.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A run that exceeds twice its expected length is \"unlucky\"; by Markov that happens with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, and five independent unlucky runs is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> We want a hard time cap but the base algorithm only bounds expected time — capping each run and repeating trades a small failure chance for a guaranteed running time, and Markov is the one tool that needs only the expectation.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Cap each run.</b> Run the algorithm but stop a run the moment its step counter hits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Repeat at most 5 times, using independent randomness each time.</li>\n<li><b>Output rule.</b> If some run finishes within its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> budget, return its (correct) answer. If all 5 are aborted, just return the first array element as a guess.</li>\n<li><b>Bound the operations.</b> Each run does at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, so 5 runs do at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; with the counter bookkeeping the total stays under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>25</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> always — a hard worst-case cap.</li>\n<li><b>Failure needs all to overshoot.</b> The answer is wrong only if every run exceeded <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, since any run that finishes returns the exact correct selection.</li>\n<li><b>Markov on one run.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, Markov gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Multiply independent runs.</b> The 5 runs use independent coins, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>all&#x000A0;5&#x000A0;overshoot</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Hence the answer is correct with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, and each extra retry cuts the failure chance in half.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2021A-p2#1": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write \"pick the most students, at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> from each course\" as a 0/1 integer program.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> give each student one yes/no variable, maximize how many are chosen, and add one capacity line per course.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> \"at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> chosen students belong to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>\" is just the linear inequality that sums the chosen students sitting in that course.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the problem is a yes/no decision per student under counting limits — the textbook shape of a binary integer program, so we translate the words straight into math.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Variables.</b> For each student <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> meaning student <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is selected. Binary values make each student an all-or-nothing choice.</li>\n  <li><b>Objective.</b> We want the largest selected group, so we maximize the count <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> (each chosen student adds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>).</li>\n  <li><b>Constraints.</b> For a course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, the chosen students who take it are those <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so \"at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> of them\" is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, one line per course.</li>\n  <li><b>Full program.</b>\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>A student in several courses appears in several <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so choosing them spends one unit of each of those caps at once — exactly the \"counts as a representative for each\" behavior the problem describes.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#2": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> put the concrete instance in matrix/vector form and write its dual BIP with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> build the \"who attends what\" matrix, write the primal Max compactly, then transpose to get the dual Min.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the dual of this packing program swaps Max<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02194;</mo></mrow></math>Min, rows<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02194;</mo></mrow></math>columns, and the caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02194;</mo></mrow></math> the all-ones right-hand side.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> once the primal is in matrix form, the dual is mechanical — transpose the matrix and flip the direction — so encoding membership as a matrix does all the work.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Membership matrix.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> (row = student, column = course):\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n  Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the count of selected attendees of course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Primal.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>,\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li><b>Dual.</b> Transposing back, the dual takes one variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> per course, minimizes total cap-cost, and each student gives a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" row:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>M</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mover><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each of the five rows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mover><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math> is one student's requirement (for instance row 1 reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) — the same covering meaning spelled out in part 3.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#3": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write the dual BIP of the general primal from part 1 and say in words what it means.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> transpose the primal — one dual variable per course, one dual constraint per student — and read off the objective and rows.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> duality turns the students' <em>packing</em> problem into a <em>covering</em> problem over courses: pick cheapest courses that reach every student.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the primal's caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> become the dual's costs and its right-hand ones become the dual's \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" demands, so transposing directly exposes a familiar set-cover.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Variables.</b> One binary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> per course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is chosen; its cost is the primal cap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Objective.</b> The primal's right-hand sides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> become dual objective coefficients and the direction flips: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the total cost of chosen courses.</li>\n  <li><b>Constraints.</b> Each student <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives one row. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> sits in exactly the courses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the row is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: every student must be enrolled in at least one chosen course.</li>\n  <li><b>Full dual.</b>\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Read plainly: choose courses of least total cost so that every student is covered by at least one — a weighted set-cover where courses are the sets, students are the elements, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the price of using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#4": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> false — the rounding \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, else <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\" can produce an infeasible integral solution.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> exhibit one small instance where the optimal fractional solution sits exactly at the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, so rounding pushes everything up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and blows a cap.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a cap of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> split evenly among <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> students gives each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> — right at the threshold — so all of them round up and the sum jumps to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> to refute a \"for every input\" claim you only need one counterexample, and the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is precisely where a balanced fractional optimum lands, making it the natural place to attack.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Simplest instance.</b> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The only constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and the fractional optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — optimal since the cap holds the sum at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Rounding breaks it.</b> The threshold is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, and both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so both round to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Now <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: the cap is violated, so the rounded solution is infeasible.</li>\n  <li><b>A tighter instance.</b> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Setting every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> is feasible (each constraint sums <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, and it is optimal because any better value would force three variables to exceed a sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Same failure.</b> Threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, and every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> rounds to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; then each course's sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, infeasible again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the rule fails exactly because a fair fractional split lands every variable on the rounding line — round them all up and each cap-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> course is overfilled.</p>\n", "2021A-p3#1": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> finish a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>-approximation for TSP on a complete graph with edge weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, where the given first step computes a minimum-cost cycle cover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> stitch the vertex-disjoint cycles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> into a single tour by rerouting one edge of each cycle to jump into the next cycle.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a cycle cover is a cheap stand-in for a tour (a tour is a cover with one cycle), so we only need to merge the pieces without paying much.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the minimum cycle cover is computable in polynomial time and lower-bounds OPT; each cycle has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices, so there are few of them and few patch edges to pay for.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Order the cycles.</b> Take the cycles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> in any order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>, writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is its closing edge.</li>\n<li><b>Bridge each cycle to the next.</b> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, delete the closing edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, continuing into the first vertex of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> instead of closing.</li>\n<li><b>Close the loop.</b> For the last cycle, replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, returning to the very first vertex.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Every replacement edge exists because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is complete, and chaining <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves one cycle through all vertices exactly once — a valid tour, whose cost is bounded in part (2).</p>\n", "2021A-p3#2": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the algorithm from part (1) returns a valid tour of cost at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> bound the extra cost of merging by counting the cycles, then compare the cycle cover and the edge count against OPT.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> each patch swaps an edge of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for one of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so it adds at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> patches.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> two clean lower bounds on OPT are available — the cover is no costlier than the optimal tour, and any tour has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> unit-or-more edges — so both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> fold into OPT.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>It is a tour.</b> Merging as in (1) yields one cycle visiting every vertex exactly once, so the output is a legal TSP tour.</li>\n<li><b>Few cycles.</b> Each cycle has at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices and they are disjoint over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices, so their count is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Cheap patching.</b> Each merge replaces an edge of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> by one of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, an increase of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> merges, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Bound by OPT.</b> The optimal tour is itself a cycle cover, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>; and any tour has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> edges each costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Chaining these: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>. So the algorithm never exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> times the best tour.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#1": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> formulate maximum semi-matching in the bipartite graph as a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> integer program.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> one binary variable per edge, maximize how many edges are chosen, and cap each vertex's degree — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> side, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> side.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a semi-matching is just a degree-capped edge set, and the only twist from ordinary matching is that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices are allowed two edges instead of one.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> every rule defining a semi-matching is a per-vertex degree bound, which is a linear inequality, so the model writes itself once you set an edge variable.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Choose variables.</b> For each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is selected.</li>\n<li><b>Objective.</b> Largest semi-matching means the most edges: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Cap the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> side.</b> Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> may touch at most one chosen edge: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Cap the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> side.</b> Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> may touch at most two: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector respects both caps, so it is a semi-matching, and its objective counts its edges — an optimal solution is a maximum semi-matching.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#2": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write the semi-matching program for the drawn graph in matrix/vector form and give its dual BIP.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> make each vertex a constraint row and each edge a column; the entry is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> when the edge touches the vertex, the right-hand side is the vertex's degree cap, then transpose for the dual.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the constraint matrix is just the vertex-edge incidence matrix, so building it is bookkeeping: mark which edges hit which vertex.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> once the abstract program from part (1) is in hand, plugging in a specific graph is only filling the incidence matrix, and the dual is its transpose by the standard recipe.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Read the graph.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Fill the rows.</b> One row per vertex: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, collected into matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Primal.</b> Caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Dual.</b> Swap: cost vector becomes the primal right-hand side, matrix transposes, direction flips: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Each dual constraint is one edge asking that at least one of its two endpoints be chosen — the vertex-cover flavor made concrete for this graph.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#3": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the general dual of the semi-matching BIP is <b>weighted vertex cover</b> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> read the dual variable per vertex as \"is this vertex chosen?\", the constraint per edge as \"cover this edge\", and the objective coefficients as the caps carried over from the primal.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the primal caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> become the dual costs, so the two sides of the bipartition get weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the dual has \"one variable per vertex, one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> per edge, minimize total\" — that shape is precisely vertex cover, so we just name it and attach the weights.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Variables.</b> One <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> per vertex, meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is put in the cover.</li>\n<li><b>Constraints.</b> Edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: at least one endpoint of every edge is chosen — the cover condition.</li>\n<li><b>Objective.</b> Costs are the primal caps: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So the dual asks for the cheapest set of vertices touching every edge, charging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> per <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> per <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex — weighted vertex cover.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#4": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> <b>False</b> — rounding every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> can produce an infeasible integer solution.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> to break a \"for all inputs\" claim, exhibit one graph where the LP optimum, when rounded this way, overloads a vertex.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> pile many edges onto a single capacity-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> vertex: each gets fractional value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, and rounding them all up blows past the cap.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> threshold rounding at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> has no built-in respect for the \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>\" constraint, so a star where four half-valued edges meet one vertex is the natural place it fails.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Pick the graph.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: four edges all meeting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Give the fractional optimum.</b> Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for all four. Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex carries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> carries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Feasible, value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Check it is optimal.</b> The constraint at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> caps the objective at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and this point hits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Round and fail.</b> Every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so all become <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; now <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, which is not a semi-matching.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The rounding turned a legal fractional optimum into an illegal integer point, so the rule does not always work — whenever many edges crowd one vertex, rounding them all up breaks its cap.</p>\n", "2021B-p3#1": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Design a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for MAX-3-COL: color <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> to maximize the total weight of legally colored edges (endpoints differently colored).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Think of the coloring as a partition of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> into three classes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, and place vertices one at a time, each into the class that adds the least illegal weight.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Putting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> in a class makes exactly its edges to already-placed vertices of that class illegal, so greedily choosing the lightest of the three classes controls the damage.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> This is the three-color version of the two-color greedy max-cut from Homework 2; whenever \"maximize agreeing/differing edges\" appears, sequential greedy placement is the natural reach.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Initialize.</b> Start with three empty color classes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Process in order.</b> Go through the vertices in an arbitrary fixed order. For the current vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and each class <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the total weight of edges from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to vertices already in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Greedy placement.</b> Add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to a class <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — this makes only the edges from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> illegal, so we add as little illegal weight as possible.</li>\n<li><b>Output the coloring.</b> Once all vertices are placed, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Coloring greedily by the lightest class is the whole algorithm.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2021B-p3#2": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> The greedy algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation: its legally colored weight is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> of the optimum.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Bound the illegal weight created at each step by an average, sum over vertices so every edge is counted once, then compare to the easy bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Choosing the lightest of three classes puts the new illegal weight at most one-third of the vertex's edges into placed vertices, so total illegal weight is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A greedy \"pick the minimum\" rule always pairs with \"min <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> average\", and charging each edge to its later endpoint is the standard way to make the per-step bounds add up cleanly.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Set up the timeline.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be the processing order and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> the classes when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is considered (only earlier vertices are in them).</li>\n<li><b>Per-vertex bound.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> goes to the class minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the minimum of three numbers is at most their average, the illegal weight created is <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Each edge once.</b> An edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is seen exactly when the later endpoint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is processed, so <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Total illegal weight.</b> Summing the per-vertex bound over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, the total illegally colored weight is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Compare to OPT.</b> So the legal weight is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Since no coloring beats all edges, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, giving legal weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> — the greedy loses at most a third of the weight.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False: the simplex algorithm from class never moves to a different vertex having the same objective value.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Recall the pivot rule: the algorithm only relocates when the move strictly improves the objective.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A \"sideways\" step to an equal-value vertex is never taken; the only place equal values show up is a degenerate pivot, and that keeps you at the same vertex.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The statement sounds like degeneracy, so the trigger is to separate \"the basis changed\" from \"the vertex changed\" — degeneracy changes the former without the latter.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>The pivot rule.</b> As presented in class, simplex leaves its current vertex only when it can strictly decrease the objective function.</li>\n<li><b>Degenerate case.</b> When the solution is degenerate, a pivot may swap which variables are in the basis, but the actual point (vertex) does not move, so its value is unchanged. This is not a move to a different vertex.</li>\n<li><b>So equal-value moves don't happen.</b> Either the objective strictly drops and you reach a genuinely new, better vertex, or the vertex stays put during a degenerate basis change. There is no step to a different vertex with the same value.</li>\n<li><b>Conclusion.</b> The described behavior contradicts the rule, so the statement is false — here a different vertex always means a strictly better value.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: the isolation lemma still holds when the weight of a set is the <b>product</b> of its element weights instead of the sum.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Take a logarithm to turn products into sums, apply the ordinary isolation lemma there, then translate the unique minimum back.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The original proof never used that weights come from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> — it only needed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> distinct values and an additive weight. A log map supplies exactly that.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Products and \"unique minimum\" scream logarithm: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> is order-preserving, so it moves the multiplicative world to the additive world where we already have the lemma.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Define the transform.</b> Given a multiplicative weight function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. This map is one-to-one, onto, and order-preserving from the original weights onto <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, still <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> distinct values.</li>\n<li><b>Products become sums.</b> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the multiplicative weight of a set matches its additive weight under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Apply the known lemma.</b> The standard isolation lemma only needs distinct additive weights, which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math> provides. So with high probability there is a unique minimum-weight set under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Translate back.</b> Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> preserves order, that same set is the unique minimum under the product weight. So the lemma carries over — nothing special about sums was ever being used.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p1#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> One inclusion is trivial; for the other, take an algorithm with a weak gap and boost it by repeating and taking a majority-style vote.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A constant gap between the accept probabilities on yes- and no-instances can be amplified to an exponentially small error by running many independent copies.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Whenever you see two BPP classes with different error thresholds, the reach is always error amplification: independent repetitions plus a Chernoff bound close the gap.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Easy inclusion.</b> A tiny error rate is stronger than a large one, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is immediate.</li>\n<li><b>Set up the weak algorithm.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> via a machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. There is a fixed gap around the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Repeat independently.</b> Run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times with fresh coins each time, and accept iff the fraction of accepting runs is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Bound the error.</b> The true accept rate sits at distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math> from the threshold in both cases, so by the Chernoff bound the empirical count is very unlikely to land on the wrong side; the error shrinks fast, below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, once we use enough repetitions.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> So the boosted algorithm places <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Both inclusions hold, so the classes are equal — a weak coin-flip advantage is really as good as an almost-certain one.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p1#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrices can be multiplied with at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>49</mn></mrow></math> multiplications.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> View each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrix as a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> blocks and run Strassen once at the block level.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Strassen multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> products instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>; applying it recursively, the block products themselves each cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> is naturally a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> grid of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> blocks, so one recursion level of Strassen is the obvious lever once you want to beat the naive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>64</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Block the matrices.</b> Split each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrix into four <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> blocks, so it looks like a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrix whose entries are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrices.</li>\n<li><b>Outer Strassen.</b> Multiplying these two block matrices with Strassen needs only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> block multiplications rather than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> (the extra additions of blocks cost no scalar multiplications).</li>\n<li><b>Inner cost.</b> Each block multiplication is a genuine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> product, again done by Strassen with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> scalar multiplications.</li>\n<li><b>Count.</b> Total scalar multiplications are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow></math>, which meets the bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>49</mn></mrow></math> — one level of recursion is exactly what buys the savings over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>64</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p2#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write an integer program for Minimum Dominating Set and justify the variables and constraints.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Use one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variable per vertex for \"selected\", minimize the total selected, and add one coverage constraint per vertex.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is dominated\" means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> or one of its neighbors is chosen, which is a single <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> inequality.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Selection problems map naturally to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indicator variables, and each \"must be covered\" requirement becomes a linear lower-bound constraint.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Variables.</b> For each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is in the dominating set. An assignment encodes the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Objective.</b> The size of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Coverage constraints.</b> Each vertex must be chosen or have a chosen neighbor:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Why it corresponds.</b> A feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> solution gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; each constraint guarantees <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> or a neighbor is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> dominates. Conversely any dominating set is feasible, so minimizing the objective finds a minimum dominating set.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Give a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for Maximum Triple Cut (partition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> maximizing edges between parts).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Assign each vertex to one of the three parts uniformly at random, compute the expected cut, then derandomize with conditional expectations.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> With three parts, a random assignment separates any fixed edge's endpoints with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, and linearity turns that into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> of all edges in expectation.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Random partitioning gives an easy per-edge probability, and the method of conditional expectations makes the guarantee deterministic without losing anything.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Randomized algorithm.</b> Independently put each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, each with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Per-edge probability.</b> Fix edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Whatever set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> lands in, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> lands in that same set with probability exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, so the edge is cut with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Expected cut.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is cut, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. By linearity of expectation, <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Ratio.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Derandomize.</b> By the method of conditional expectations, fix vertices one at a time, each time choosing the set that keeps the conditional expected cut highest. This never drops below the starting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so the final deterministic partition cuts at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> edges.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p4#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Relax the maximum-matching integer program to an LP and write it in standard form (minimize, equalities, nonnegative variables).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Drop the integrality constraint, flip the objective to a minimization, and add a slack variable to each inequality.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Standard form needs equalities, so every \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" constraint gets its own nonnegative slack that absorbs the leftover.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Form suitable for simplex\" is code for standard form, and the only two things not yet in that shape are the max objective and the inequality constraints.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Max to min.</b> Turn <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>; negating the objective keeps the same optimizer.</li>\n  <li><b>Inequalities to equalities.</b> For each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, add a slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> just measures how far <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is from being fully matched.</li>\n  <li><b>Write it compactly.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></math> the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> incidence matrix (column <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in rows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>), the LP is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Negate the objective and let one slack per vertex soak up the slack, and the matching LP is already in the shape simplex wants.</p>\n", "2023A-p4#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write the dual of the standard-form matching LP from part (a), simplified as much as possible.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Apply the standard dual rule, split the constraint by the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>-block and the slack-block, then flip signs to clean it up.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The slack columns are the identity, so their dual constraints simply bound the dual variables' sign.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Every equality-constrained primal has a mechanical dual, and the only work left is reading the two column-blocks and tidying the signs.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>One variable per equality.</b> The primal has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> equality constraints, so the dual has a free vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Split the blocks.</b> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>-columns give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>; the slack columns give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Flip signs to simplify.</b> Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>: the objective becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, the constraint becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The clean dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. per edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2023A-p4#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> Adding integrality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> to the dual LP from part (b) gives the minimum vertex cover problem.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Read the integral variables as \"pick this vertex,\" then interpret the edge constraint and the objective.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> values says every edge has at least one chosen endpoint, which is exactly a vertex cover.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Once variables are forced to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, an LP becomes a combinatorial selection problem, so we just translate each piece back into graph language.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Read the variables.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> be the chosen vertices.</li>\n  <li><b>Read the constraint.</b> For edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> forces at least one endpoint into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> touches every edge, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex cover.</li>\n  <li><b>Read the objective.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, the smallest such cover, so this is minimum vertex cover.</li>\n  <li><b>Duality picture.</b> The primal was fractional maximum matching and this dual is fractional minimum vertex cover, so LP duality gives max fractional matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> min fractional vertex cover.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">For bipartite graphs the LPs even have integral optima, so matching and cover values coincide, which is Konig's theorem falling right out of duality.</p>\n", "2023A-p5#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> give an efficient randomized algorithm for Matrix Completion (max achievable rank), with correctness, runtime, and error probability.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> replace each star by a random integer and return the rank; argue via Schwartz&ndash;Zippel that a random substitution almost surely realizes the best possible rank.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the max rank is achievable iff some submatrix determinant can be made nonzero, and that determinant is a low-degree polynomial in the starred entries, so a random plug-in rarely hits its roots.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the hint \"determinants and rank\" plus starred entries acting as variables is the classic signal to reach for Schwartz&ndash;Zippel.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Algorithm.</b> For each starred entry, independently plug in a uniform random integer from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; compute the rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> of the numeric matrix by Gaussian elimination and return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>. This is polynomial time.</li>\n<li><b>Name the target.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of stars and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the true optimum, achieved by some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, there are rows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and columns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, whose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> submatrix has nonzero determinant.</li>\n<li><b>Build the polynomial.</b> Treat the stars as variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>; the determinant of that submatrix is a polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>. It is not identically zero, since at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> it equals the nonzero determinant above. Each entry has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Schwartz&ndash;Zippel and conclusion.</b> A nonzero degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial evaluated at uniform points from a size-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> set is zero with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>; here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so the failure probability is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Thus with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> the random matrix still has that nonzero determinant, giving rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>; and rank can never exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, so the algorithm returns exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>. Error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, runtime polynomial.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p6#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time compute the sumset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> and, for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, how many pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sum to it.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Encode each set as an indicator polynomial, multiply the two, and read off the coefficients.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> in the product counts exactly the pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"How many ways does each value arise as a sum\" is a convolution, and convolution is polynomial multiplication — which FFT does in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Encode as polynomials.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></math>; the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is in the set.</li>\n<li><b>Multiply.</b> In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, where each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> precisely when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Interpret.</b> So that coefficient counts the ways to write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> exactly when it is positive, and the value is the number of representations.</li>\n<li><b>Cost.</b> Both polynomials have degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so FFT multiplies them in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; building them and scanning the coefficients is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023B-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — this is exactly strong duality.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Recognize the hypothesis as the setup of the strong duality theorem and quote its conclusion.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> If the primal is feasible (\"non-empty\") and its objective can't run off to infinity (\"bounded\"), then the dual is feasible too and both optima are equal.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Any statement equating primal and dual optimal values over a nice feasible region is a duality question, so reach straight for the duality theorem.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Primal has a finite optimum.</b> Non-empty means a feasible point exists; bounded means the objective doesn't go to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>. So the primal optimum is a finite number.</li>\n<li><b>Weak duality orders them.</b> For any feasible primal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, the dual value is at most the primal value, so the dual can't be unbounded.</li>\n<li><b>Strong duality closes the gap.</b> The theorem then guarantees the dual is feasible and the two optimal values are equal, not just ordered.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The parenthetical is the same idea in reverse: an infeasible dual would force an unbounded primal, which can't happen here.</p>\n", "2023B-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — one pivoting step of simplex runs in polynomial time.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> List what happens in a single pivot and note each piece is cheap linear algebra.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The claim is about the cost of one pivot, not how many pivots the whole run takes (that count can be exponential).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Polynomial per step\" is decided by opening up the step into its operations, so we just itemize a pivot.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Pick the entering variable.</b> Compute the reduced-cost vector and choose a coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> whose reduced cost shows the objective can improve. This is a matrix-vector computation on the input data.</li>\n<li><b>Pick the leaving variable.</b> Using the entering column, the ratio test scans polynomially many ratios and selects a coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> to drop from the basis.</li>\n<li><b>Update the basis.</b> Swapping <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> in and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> out needs a basis-inverse recompute and the new basic solution — some matrix inversions and multiplications, each polynomial in the input dimensions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Every ingredient of a pivot is a standard polynomial-time routine, so the step is polynomial. The hard part of simplex is the possible number of steps, not the price of one.</p>\n", "2023B-p1#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False — no nonzero 5-variable polynomial vanishes at every integer point.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Use Schwartz–Zippel on a small integer grid to produce a point where the polynomial is nonzero.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A nonzero degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial has very few roots on a grid, so a non-root must exist among finitely many integer points.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Does a nonzero polynomial have a non-root?\" is precisely what Schwartz–Zippel answers, so it is the natural tool.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Set up.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be nonzero of total degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>; we want an integer point where it doesn't vanish.</li>\n<li><b>Choose a grid.</b> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and consider assignments in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Apply Schwartz–Zippel.</b> For a uniform point of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> Since the chance of hitting a root is below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Those <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math>'s are integers, so we found an integer assignment where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> is nonzero — a nonzero polynomial cannot vanish everywhere, hence the statement is false.</p>\n", "2023B-p1#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — such a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> exists, by the spectral mixing of random walks.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Track the walk's distribution in the eigenbasis; the small second eigenvalue makes it converge to uniform in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> steps.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The uniform direction is fixed by the walk, and every other direction shrinks by at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> each step, so after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> steps the distribution is essentially <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A gap between the top eigenvalue and the rest is the signature of an expander, and the standard reach for \"reaches everywhere fast\" is the spectral mixing argument from class.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Set up the walk.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the normalized adjacency (transition) matrix; start at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> with indicator vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. After <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps the distribution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Split into uniform plus remainder.</b> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> as its uniform component plus a part orthogonal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>. Applying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> keeps the uniform part (eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) and shrinks the rest by at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is tiny, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> with error norm below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The chance of being at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>-th entry, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. So the walk reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2023B-p2#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> Weak duality: for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and its dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Chain the dual constraint through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, then substitute the primal equality.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Multiplying the dual inequality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> by the nonnegative <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> keeps the inequality, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> collapses the left side to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The primal objective involves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and the dual involves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>; the only bridge between them is the constraint pair, so we combine them directly.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Use dual feasibility with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> coordinatewise and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, multiplying by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup></mrow></math> preserves the direction: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Use primal feasibility.</b> Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> into the left side: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Read off the result.</b> These are scalars and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, the claim.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The one place nonnegativity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> matters is step 1: that is exactly what lets the dual inequality survive being multiplied through, so every dual value sits below every primal value.</p>\n", "2023B-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> give an efficient algorithm that finds a vertex cover at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> times the optimum in a 3-uniform hypergraph, where every edge is a triple <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and a cover must hit every triple.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> repeatedly grab an uncovered edge, throw all three of its vertices into the cover, and delete every edge they touch; then charge the cost against a set of disjoint edges.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the edges we pick are pairwise disjoint, and any cover must spend at least one distinct vertex on each of them — so the optimum is at least the number of picks, while we spend exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices per pick.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> this is the same maximal-matching trick behind the classic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for vertex cover in ordinary graphs; edges here hold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so the ratio simply climbs from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Greedy loop.</b> Start with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>. While edges remain: pick any edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, add all three vertices to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, and erase every edge that contains at least one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>. Each round removes at least the picked edge, so this finishes in polynomial time.</li>\n<li><b>What we spent.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of rounds. Each round adds exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>The picked edges are disjoint.</b> When an edge is picked, every other edge sharing a vertex with it is erased, so a later pick shares no vertex with the earlier ones. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> picked edges are therefore pairwise vertex-disjoint.</li>\n<li><b>Lower bound on the optimum.</b> Any valid cover must contain a vertex from each picked edge, and since these <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> edges share no vertices, that forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> <em>distinct</em> vertices. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Put it together.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, so the algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>There's also an LP version: write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> per edge, relax <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, solve, and round <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Every edge's three values sum to at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so one of them is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> and gets rounded up — the edge stays covered — and each rounded-up variable had <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so the cost grows by at most a factor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> either way, which is the intuition to hold onto: a triple simply costs you three where a pair cost two.</p>\n", "2023B-p4#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> Every Hamiltonian cycle gives a feasible solution to the degree-constrained TSP integer program.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Encode the cycle by setting each variable to whether its edge is used, then check both degree constraints.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A Hamiltonian cycle enters and leaves each vertex exactly once, which is precisely what the constraints ask.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The IP variables are edge indicators, so the natural candidate solution is just the indicator vector of the cycle's edges.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Build the solution.</b> Given a Hamiltonian cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math>, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math>, else <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. These are in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, meeting the integrality constraint.</li>\n<li><b>Recall the degree constraints.</b> For every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the incoming edges must sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and the outgoing edges must sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Use the cycle structure.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> visits each vertex exactly once as a single closed tour, so at each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> it enters through one edge and leaves through one edge.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude feasibility.</b> Thus each incoming sum and each outgoing sum equals exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so all constraints hold and the indicator vector is feasible. (Its objective value is exactly the cycle's weight, which is why the IP models TSP.)</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023B-p4#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> The converse fails — a feasible IP solution need not be a Hamiltonian cycle.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Exhibit a graph whose edges split into two disjoint cycles, each obeying the degree constraints.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The degree constraints only force in-degree and out-degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; they never force a single connected tour.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The hint points at connectivity — the fastest counterexample is a disconnected union of small cycles, which no degree constraint can rule out.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Pick the graph.</b> On six vertices take two vertex-disjoint directed triangles, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>; set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on those six edges, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> elsewhere.</li>\n<li><b>Check feasibility.</b> In each triangle every vertex has exactly one chosen incoming and one chosen outgoing edge, so every incoming and outgoing sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and all variables are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Feasible.</li>\n<li><b>Show it isn't Hamiltonian.</b> A Hamiltonian cycle is one cycle through all vertices, but here the vertices split into two separate cycles — the subgraph isn't even connected.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">This is the classic subtour phenomenon: the degree constraints allow any collection of disjoint cycles covering all vertices, which is exactly the gap part (c) fixes.</p>\n", "2023B-p4#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> With the subtour-elimination constraints added, every feasible IP solution is a Hamiltonian cycle.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Assume a feasible solution were more than one cycle, then show the new constraint on one cycle's vertex set is violated.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A cycle on a proper vertex subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math> uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> internal edges, but the constraint caps that at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Part (b) showed the only failure mode is disjoint subtours, so the natural fix is a constraint that forbids any cycle from closing inside a proper subset.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Start from the structure.</b> The degree constraints already force any feasible solution to be a vertex-disjoint union of directed cycles covering all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Suppose two or more cycles.</b> Pick one cycle and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> be its vertex set; since another cycle exists, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0228A;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> is a proper subset.</li>\n<li><b>Count its internal edges.</b> That cycle uses exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> edges, all with both endpoints in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Hit the contradiction.</b> The subtour constraint for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> demands this be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — contradiction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So no cycle can close on a proper subset; the only option left is one cycle through all vertices — a Hamiltonian cycle. With part (a), the correspondence is now exact.</p>\n", "2023B-p5#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> compute the transitive closure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (which pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are joined by some directed path) in total time asymptotically below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> turn reachability into a single matrix power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, then compute that power fast using fast matrix multiplication together with repeated squaring.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> powers of the adjacency matrix count walks — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the number of length-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> walks from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> — and adding a self-loop at every vertex turns \"length exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>\" into \"length at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>\".</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> plain reachability (BFS from every vertex) already costs about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>; to beat that we lean on fast matrix multiplication, which runs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, so we reshape the problem into matrix multiplications.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Walks from powers.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. A fact from class: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> counts the walks of length <em>exactly</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff such a walk exists.</li>\n<li><b>Self-loops relax \"exactly\" to \"at most\".</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>, the adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> obtained by adding a self-loop at every vertex. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a path from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> of length <em>at most</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>: a self-loop lets us pad any shorter walk out to length exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Pick the exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</b> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> can reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> at all, it can along a simple path, which visits at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices and so has length at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Hence reachability is captured exactly by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>: put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>The algorithm.</b> Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>; compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> by repeated squaring — about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math> squarings, each one a fast matrix multiplication; then read <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> off the positive entries.</li>\n<li><b>Running time.</b> Forming <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> and reading off <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> each. The power needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> matrix multiplications at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> apiece, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> total. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, this is below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Repeated squaring is what keeps us cheap: instead of multiplying by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> times, we square <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times to reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, and each square rides on fast matrix multiplication to stay under the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> barrier.</p>\n", "2023B-p6#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> give a randomized coloring with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> with high probability.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> color every element red/blue independently at random; bound one set's discrepancy with Chernoff, then union-bound over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> sets.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> sum concentrates tightly around <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so a single set's discrepancy exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only with tiny probability.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> discrepancy is exactly the absolute value of a sum of independent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> terms, which is the textbook setup for a Chernoff/Hoeffding tail bound.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Encode colors as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</b> Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for red, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for blue, chosen independently and uniformly. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and a set's discrepancy is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>red</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>blue</mtext><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Fix one set.</b> Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>, exceeding the larger threshold implies exceeding the smaller: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Chernoff on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> sum.</b> Using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>, the exponent is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Picking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> large enough drives this below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> &mdash; the error shrinks fast as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> grows.</li>\n<li><b>Union bound.</b> Over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> sets, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. So with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> every set is below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>, and one simple random coloring does the job.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024A-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False: if a standard-form LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and its dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are both feasible, the optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> cannot be strictly greater than the optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Quote strong duality, which forces the two optimal values to be equal.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> When both programs are feasible there is no duality gap, so a strict inequality is impossible.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Any T/F about the gap between a primal and its dual optimum is really a question about which duality theorem applies, and \"both feasible\" is exactly the trigger for strong duality.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Set the convention.</b> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, with dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Weak duality gives one direction.</b> Always <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the only question is whether the inequality can be strict.</li>\n  <li><b>Strong duality closes the gap.</b> If both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are feasible, both optima are finite and equal: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Conclude.</b> Equal values leave no room for \"strictly greater,\" so the statement is false.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Once both sides are feasible, the primal and dual meet at the same value, so neither can beat the other.</p>\n", "2024A-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False: a symmetric matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> need not be PSD.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Give one symmetric matrix with nonnegative determinant that is clearly not PSD.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The determinant is the product of eigenvalues, so an even number of negative eigenvalues keeps it positive while the matrix is far from PSD.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A claimed implication is fastest to kill with a counterexample, and \"determinant vs. eigenvalues\" points straight at a matrix with two negative eigenvalues.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Pick the matrix.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>, which is symmetric.</li>\n  <li><b>Check the hypothesis.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the assumption holds.</li>\n  <li><b>Fail PSD.</b> Its eigenvalues are both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is not PSD (in fact negative definite).</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The determinant only sees the product of eigenvalues, so two negatives cancel in the sign and hide a matrix that is the opposite of PSD.</p>\n", "2024A-p1#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: there is a polynomial-time algorithm that, given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, finds an orthonormal basis of their span.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Run Gram-Schmidt to get an orthogonal basis, then normalize, and note every step is a cheap vector computation.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Subtracting from each vector its projections onto the earlier ones makes them mutually orthogonal.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Orthonormal basis of a span\" is the textbook job of Gram-Schmidt, and the only thing to check is that it runs in polynomial time.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Reduce to independent vectors.</b> If the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are dependent, greedily keep a maximal independent subset (each independence test is a rank computation, polynomial time); it spans the same subspace.</li>\n  <li><b>Orthogonalize.</b> Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Check orthogonality.</b> The coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is chosen exactly so that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Normalize.</b> Divide each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> by its length to get unit vectors, giving an orthonormal basis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The whole thing is just a bounded number of inner products and subtractions, so it finishes quickly and hands back the basis we wanted.</p>\n", "2024A-p1#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: if every reduced cost in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> is strictly positive at a basic feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is the unique optimum.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Write any feasible point's cost in terms of the reduced costs, then read off both optimality and uniqueness.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Moving away from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> means turning on some nonbasic coordinate, and every such move strictly raises the cost because its reduced cost is positive.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Reduced costs are precisely the per-unit cost change of entering a nonbasic variable, so a positivity assumption on them is a direct handle on optimality.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Value at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Value at any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Expressing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> through the basis gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the reduced-cost vector.</li>\n  <li><b>Optimality.</b> Feasibility forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Uniqueness.</b> If any coordinate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> were positive, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>; so an optimum needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Any step off <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> has to switch on a nonbasic variable, and each of those only makes the cost worse, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> stands alone at the bottom.</p>\n", "2024A-p2#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Build a randomized polynomial-time algorithm estimating a CNF formula's density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, correct with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Sample many random assignments, output the fraction that satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math>, and use a concentration bound to fix the sample size.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is exactly the probability a uniformly random assignment satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math>, so a sample average estimates it.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Whenever a quantity is a probability over a huge set you can't scan, Monte-Carlo sampling plus a Chernoff bound is the standard way to estimate it cheaply and reliably.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>The algorithm.</b> Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> independent uniform assignments <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, evaluate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for each (fast, linear in the formula size), and output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the fraction that satisfy.</li>\n<li><b>It aims at the right value.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is uniform, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — the estimate is centered correctly.</li>\n<li><b>Concentration.</b> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent and in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so by the Chernoff bound the chance the estimate is off by more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> shrinks fast as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> grows.</li>\n<li><b>Pick the sample size.</b> Choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> large enough that this error drops below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>; then the algorithm succeeds with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> fast evaluations it runs in polynomial time.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024A-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> with weights drawn uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math>, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> every pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> has a unique minimum-weight path.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> handle one pair at a time: bound the chance a single pair has a tie using the isolation lemma, then union-bound over all pairs.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the isolation lemma says that if you give items random weights from a large enough range, the minimum-weight subset out of any family of subsets (here, the lightest path among all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> paths) comes out unique with high probability — and the failure chance shrinks as the range grows.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"unique minimum\" over a possibly huge family of objects (there can be exponentially many paths) is exactly what the isolation lemma is built for: its bound depends only on the number of edges, not on how many paths there are.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Set the range.</b> Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math>, and give every edge an independent uniform weight from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Fix one pair.</b> For vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be the family of all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> paths, each viewed as its set of edges. The number of edges is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Apply the isolation lemma.</b> With random weights from a range of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> elements, the minimum-weight member of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is unique except with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Call the pair <b>bad</b> if its lightest path is not unique.</li>\n<li><b>Union bound over pairs.</b> There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> pairs, so the chance that <em>some</em> pair is bad is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Read off the guarantee.</b> Hence with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> no pair is bad — every pair has a single lightest path, as required.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The whole trick is that one random weighting quietly breaks all the ties at once: each pair is safe with room to spare, and even after adding up the tiny failure chances over every pair we stay comfortably above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2024A-p4#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write an integer linear program for weighted Max-SAT and justify the variables and constraints.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Use one binary variable per Boolean variable and one per clause, cap each clause variable by a linear count of its satisfied literals, and maximize total satisfied weight.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A clause is satisfied exactly when at least one of its literals is true, and \"at least one\" is captured by bounding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> above by the number of true literals.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> To turn a logical formula into an IP, encode truth values as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and translate \"clause satisfied\" into a linear inequality — the standard SAT-to-IP recipe.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Variables.</b> For each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> true); for each clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> meant to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is satisfied. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> be the variables appearing positively, negatively in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>The program.</b>\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li><b>Right side counts true literals.</b> A positive literal contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> (true iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> true); a negative literal contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> (true iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> false). So the sum is the number of satisfied literals in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Constraint links <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> to satisfaction.</b> If no literal is satisfied the sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, forcing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; if some literal is satisfied the sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, permitting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in a maximization, the optimum sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> whenever allowed, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is satisfied.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Every choice of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is just a truth assignment, and the objective then sums the weights of the satisfied clauses — so maximizing it solves weighted Max-SAT.</p>\n", "2024A-p6#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> there is a polynomial-time randomized algorithm deciding whether the span of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> contains an invertible matrix.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> plug random values into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and check invertibility; correctness rests on the determinant being a low-degree polynomial that is nonzero somewhere exactly when an invertible combination exists.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> \"some combination is invertible\" is the same as \"the polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is not the zero polynomial\", so this is a polynomial-identity test.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> invertibility is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, a polynomial condition, and testing whether a polynomial is identically zero is exactly what a random evaluation plus Schwartz–Zippel handles.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Algorithm.</b> Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; answer \"yes\" iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is invertible (a determinant check, polynomial time).</li>\n<li><b>The polynomial.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its entries are linear in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and a determinant has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is invertible iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>No false \"yes\".</b> If the span has no invertible matrix then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, every tested matrix is singular, and we correctly say \"no\".</li>\n<li><b>Rare false \"no\".</b> If an invertible matrix exists then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is a nonzero degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> polynomial, and by Schwartz–Zippel over a set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So the algorithm answers \"yes\" with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> when an invertible matrix exists and never errs otherwise; repeating a few times drives the error way down.</p>\n", "2024B-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: a polynomial-time algorithm for integer linear programs would imply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00050;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Encode an NP-complete problem (maximum independent set) as an ILP; a fast ILP solver would then solve it fast, forcing the collapse.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> ILP is NP-hard because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variables plus linear constraints can express NP-complete problems, and it is itself in NP.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Would a fast algorithm imply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00050;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math>\" is answered by exhibiting a reduction from a known NP-complete problem into the problem in question.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Set up variables.</b> Given a graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, introduce a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for each vertex, meaning \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is chosen\".</li>\n<li><b>Encode independence.</b> For each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so no two adjacent vertices are both chosen; the chosen set is independent.</li>\n<li><b>Objective.</b> Maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>; the optimum equals the maximum independent set size, and this ILP is built in polynomial time.</li>\n<li><b>Collapse.</b> Since maximum independent set is NP-complete and ILP is in NP, a polynomial-time ILP solver would decide an NP-hard problem in polynomial time, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00050;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024B-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: there is an efficient algorithm that, given a standard-form LP and a point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, decides whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Check feasibility, then apply the class characterization of a vertex via linear independence of the support columns, which reduces to a rank computation.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Being a vertex is a purely algebraic condition — the columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> on the positive coordinates of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> must be linearly independent — and rank is polynomial to compute.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Is this point a vertex\" invites the standard-form vertex test from class, turning a geometric question into a rank check you can actually run.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Feasibility check.</b> For the polytope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, verify <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; both are trivially polynomial, and if either fails <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is not a vertex.</li>\n<li><b>Vertex characterization.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the submatrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> with the columns where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. From class, a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> has full column rank (those columns are independent).</li>\n<li><b>Compute the rank.</b> Run Gaussian elimination on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> and compare its rank to the number of positive coordinates; equality means full column rank.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex exactly when it is feasible and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> has full column rank — each step polynomial, so the test is efficient.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024B-p1#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle></mrow></math> can be computed efficiently from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> without finding the eigenvalues.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Recognize the sum as an elementary symmetric polynomial of the eigenvalues, which is a coefficient of the characteristic polynomial we already know how to compute.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Symmetric functions of eigenvalues show up directly as characteristic-polynomial coefficients, so you never need the eigenvalues themselves.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A sum over all size-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> subsets of products of eigenvalues is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and \"sum/product of roots\" always points to the coefficient relations of the polynomial with those roots.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Name the expression.</b> The sum is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th elementary symmetric polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Relate to the characteristic polynomial.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, Vieta's formulas make the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Use the known algorithm.</b> We saw in class an efficient algorithm that computes all coefficients of the characteristic polynomial of a given matrix directly.</li>\n<li><b>Read off the answer.</b> Take the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> and multiply by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, i.e. the desired sum — computed efficiently and with no eigenvalues needed.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024B-p2#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> The Walsh–Hadamard transform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> can be computed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Split <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> in half, use the recursive block structure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> so only two half-size transforms are needed, then combine them cheaply.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The two block-rows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> reuse the <em>same</em> two subproducts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so one add and one subtract build the whole answer.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A matrix defined by self-similar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> blocks is the FFT signal: divide and conquer, and shared subproducts collapse four multiplications into two.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Split.</b> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, each half of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Block formula.</b> Using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo></mrow></math>,\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n  Only the two products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> appear.</li>\n  <li><b>Recurse and combine.</b> Recursively compute those two half-size transforms, then form the top half by adding and the bottom half by subtracting — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> work.</li>\n  <li><b>Base case.</b> At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo></mrow></math> is applied directly in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The cost satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the same recurrence as merge sort and FFT, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2024B-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> A graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> minimum cuts.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Use Karger's guarantee that each fixed min cut survives with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, then add these probabilities.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> One run returns a single cut, so the events \"returns this min cut\" are disjoint and their probabilities can only sum to at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The hint points to Karger, and a lower bound per cut plus disjoint events is the standard way to turn a success probability into a counting bound.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Per-cut guarantee.</b> Fix any minimum cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As shown in class, one run of Karger's contraction returns it with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, and this holds for every minimum cut.</li>\n  <li><b>Events are disjoint.</b> A single run outputs exactly one cut, so \"returns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" for distinct cuts are mutually exclusive, and returning some min cut has probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Sum over all min cuts.</b> Letting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the set of minimum cuts, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>returns&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Rearrange.</b> This gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Since each min cut claims its own slice of at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> probability and the slices cannot overlap, there is only room for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> of them.</p>\n", "2024B-p4#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> compute the diameter of a strongly connected digraph in time asymptotically below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> turn \"reachable within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps\" into positivity of a matrix power, then binary-search the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> that connects all pairs, using fast matrix multiplication.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>, the entry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, so the diameter is the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> making <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> all-positive.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> distances become an all-or-nothing positivity test that is monotone in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, which invites binary search, and matrix powers cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> — beating the naive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Reachability via powers.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> counts length-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> walks; adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> lets walks \"wait\", so with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff there is a walk of length at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Bounded diameter.</b> Strong connectivity makes every distance finite, and a shortest path repeats no vertex, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; the diameter is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Binary search.</b> The diameter is the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> with all entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math> positive. Test <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math> is all-positive the diameter is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, else <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>; recurse on the right half of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. That is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> tests.</li>\n<li><b>Cost.</b> Each power comes from repeated squaring in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and scanning for positivity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; precomputing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> once lets each search step reuse a stored power, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> overall.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is comfortably below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>, so the diameter is found faster than the naive all-pairs approach.</p>\n", "2024B-p5#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> give a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-approximation for minimum-weight representative set (hit every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>) by relaxing and rounding the given ILP.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> solve the LP relaxation, then take every element whose fractional value is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> elements whose LP values sum to at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so one of them is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> — the threshold that guarantees <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is hit.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the LP optimum lower-bounds OPT, and the bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> hands you the exact rounding threshold, so relax-and-round is the natural approach.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Relax and solve.</b> Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, solve the LP for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Round.</b> Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, else <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> be the resulting set.</li>\n<li><b><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> is representative.</b> Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Its constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is a sum of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> terms, so their average is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>; some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> hits every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Weight bound.</b> For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>: true if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Summing with weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> is a valid representative set of weight at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> times optimal, found in polynomial time.</p>\n", "2024B-p6#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> build, in poly time, a bipartite graph that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-balanced with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> include each possible edge independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; show every subset pair has close-to-ideal edge count via Chernoff, then union-bound over all pairs.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> for fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is a sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> independent coin flips with mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, so it concentrates tightly; the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> makes each subset's tiny failure probability survive the huge number of subsets.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"for every pair of subsets, an edge count near its average\" is a demand for concentration over exponentially many events — exactly the Chernoff-plus-union-bound pattern.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Algorithm & expectation.</b> For every pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math>, add the edge independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an edge, so its expectation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. For fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> has mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> by linearity.</li>\n  <li><b>Chernoff for one pair.</b> The allowed deviation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> is a constant fraction of the mean, so Chernoff gives a fast-shrinking tail: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for an absolute constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Union bound over pairs of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</b> There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> choices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>, so the chance any of them fails is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Writing the exponent as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> large enough, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> beats <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, driving this below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for every such <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Union bound over sizes.</b> Summing over the at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> gives total failure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. So with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> the graph is balanced, and generating it is just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> coin flips — poly time. The takeaway: random edges are balanced almost surely, and requiring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is exactly what lets one bound beat all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> subsets at once.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024C-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: a standard-form polytope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> vertices.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Use the class characterization of vertices by their support, then count how many supports are possible.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A vertex is pinned down by which coordinates are nonzero, and there are only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> possible sets of nonzero coordinates.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"At most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>\" screams \"one per subset of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> things,\" so we look for a way to attach each vertex to a subset of the columns.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Recall the vertex test.</b> From class, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the submatrix of columns where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, has full column rank (those columns are linearly independent).</li>\n  <li><b>A vertex is fixed by its support.</b> If two vertices had the same support <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, both would solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>; independent columns make that solution unique, so the two vertices coincide. Hence \"which coordinates are positive\" determines the vertex.</li>\n  <li><b>Count the supports.</b> The support is a subset of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> columns, and an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-element set has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> subsets.</li>\n  <li><b>Conclude.</b> Distinct vertices give distinct subsets, so there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> vertices.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Every vertex is tagged by \"which coordinates are on,\" and there are only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> such tags, so that many is the ceiling.</p>\n", "2024C-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: for a nonzero polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0211A;</mi></mrow></math>, some point in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Bound the chance a random point from that grid is a root, and show it stays below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> If the fraction of roots is strictly less than everything, a non-root must exist.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A grid of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> per variable against a degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial is the exact setup Schwartz-Zippel was built for.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Sample from the grid.</b> Draw each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Apply Schwartz-Zippel.</b> The lemma bounds the chance of hitting a root: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Note it is below one.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the event <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has strictly positive probability.</li>\n  <li><b>Existence follows.</b> A positive-probability event over a finite grid cannot be empty, so some concrete <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> exists.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">A nonzero polynomial simply cannot vanish everywhere on a grid this wide; some grid point escapes being a root.</p>\n", "2024C-p1#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Show one class contains a language the other cannot, so they differ.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> With accept and reject thresholds both at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, a pure coin flip \"decides\" any language, so that class is everything, including undecidable ones.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Equal thresholds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> leave no real gap to exploit, so the class collapses to something trivial, a clear tell that the equality fails.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>The degenerate class is huge.</b> Take any language <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, even the halting problem. The algorithm that flips one fair coin and accepts on heads accepts every input with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, meeting both the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> bounds no matter what. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains all languages.</li>\n  <li><b>The real class is limited.</b> A language in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has a genuine gap, so we can enumerate all coin-toss strings, compute the exact acceptance probability, and answer \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>\" iff it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. This always halts and decides, so this class holds only decidable languages.</li>\n  <li><b>Compare.</b> The first class contains undecidable languages while the second contains none, so they are not equal.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Matching thresholds give you nothing to work with, so that \"class\" is trivial, while a true gap keeps you inside the decidable world.</p>\n", "2024C-p1#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: for a bipartite graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>, whether the number of perfect matchings is odd can be decided efficiently.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Compare the matching count (the permanent) with the determinant, and notice they agree once we work mod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Mod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> the sign <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> disappears, so the determinant equals the matching count in parity.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The permanent is hard to compute, but it differs from the determinant only by per-term signs, and signs vanish mod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, turning a hard count into an easy determinant.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Count matchings.</b> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> biadjacency matrix (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if left <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> joins right <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>), each perfect matching is a permutation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math> using only real edges, so their number is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Write the determinant.</b> The determinant is the same sum with signs: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Kill the signs mod 2.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the two sums match modulo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when the number of perfect matchings is odd.</li>\n  <li><b>Compute efficiently.</b> The determinant is computable in polynomial time (e.g. Gaussian elimination), so output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Parity forgives the signs, so the easy determinant hands us the odd-or-even answer the hard count would have given.</p>\n", "2024C-p2#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Compute the boolean product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02218;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Cast the boolean OR-of-ANDs as an ordinary integer matrix product, run fast matrix multiplication, then threshold each entry at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The boolean entry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02218;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when the integer inner product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is positive.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Fast matrix multiplication only exists over rings, not for boolean OR/AND directly — so we borrow it by turning the logic into arithmetic.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Logic to arithmetic.</b> Treat entries as integers. For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02227;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> equals the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and the OR over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> is true iff the sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is strictly positive.</li>\n<li><b>Equivalence.</b> So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02218;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is the standard integer product (a nonnegative sum that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> only if every term is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li><b>Multiply fast.</b> Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> with fast matrix multiplication in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2.37</mn></mrow></math>); this dominates the cost.</li>\n<li><b>Threshold.</b> Output the matrix with entry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise, scanning all entries in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, as required.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024C-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Give a randomized algorithm that, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>, finds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Just pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> uniformly at random; show each fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is close only with tiny probability, then union bound over all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> sits at expected distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> from any fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, and Chernoff makes a drop to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> exponentially unlikely — small enough to survive a polynomial-size union bound.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Avoid every member of a not-too-large set with good probability\" is the classic Chernoff-plus-union-bound pattern: bound one bad event sharply, then sum over all of them.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Algorithm.</b> Set each coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Expected distance.</b> Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is fixed and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a fair coin, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>One <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is rarely close.</b> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent, so Chernoff applies. Being at distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> means dropping <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> below the mean, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> — the error shrinks fast.</li>\n  <li><b>Union bound.</b> Failure means some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is too close, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>fail</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is swamped by the exponentially small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so for large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> the failure probability is below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math> and the random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> works with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2024C-p4#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write an ILP for bin packing and explain why every feasible solution is a valid packing.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> candidate bins with a \"used\" variable each, plus an assignment variable per item-bin pair, and tie capacity to the used-flag.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> the single constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>B</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> does double duty: it caps a used bin at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> and forbids putting anything into an unused bin.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> since one item per bin is always feasible, at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> bins are ever needed, so indexing bins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> loses nothing and makes the model finite.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Variables.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: is bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> used. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: is item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> placed in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Objective.</b> Minimize the number of used bins: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Each item placed once.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> forces exactly one bin to hold it.</li>\n  <li><b>Capacity tied to \"used\".</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>B</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> this reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> (respect capacity); if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> it reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which (weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) forces all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> — nothing may go into an unused bin.</li>\n  <li><b>Why it's valid.</b> The placement constraints put each item in exactly one bin; the capacity constraints keep each used bin within weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>; any bin holding an item is forced to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> truly counts bins used. Conversely any real packing gives a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of equal cost, so the ILP optimum equals the minimum number of bins.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024C-p6#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is infeasible and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is feasible, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is unbounded.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> use Farkas to extract an infeasibility direction, add multiples of it to the given dual point, and show the objective runs to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math> while staying feasible.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Farkas turns \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has no solution\" into a concrete vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> that is a feasible direction pointing straight uphill for the dual.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> infeasibility of a linear system is exactly what Farkas' Lemma converts into a usable certificate, which is the natural bridge from \"no primal\" to \"unbounded dual\".</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Get the certificate.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is infeasible, Farkas gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Move along it.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> be the given feasible dual point and set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Stay feasible.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> (feasibility of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; adding gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Objective blows up.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, and since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> is positive and grows without bound as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math> over feasible points, meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is unbounded.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025A-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — every 3-CNF with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> clauses has an assignment satisfying at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> clauses.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Compute the average number of satisfied clauses over all assignments, then argue some assignment must sit at or below that average.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A random assignment satisfies exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> clauses in expectation, and no quantity is entirely above its own average.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"There exists an assignment with property X\" is the classic cue for the averaging (probabilistic) argument — bound the average, then a real assignment must reach it.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Expected count.</b> Set each variable true or false by a fair coin. A 3-clause fails only when all three literals are false, probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, so it is satisfied with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math> be the number of satisfied clauses; by linearity of expectation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Average forces a witness.</b> If every assignment satisfied strictly more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, a contradiction. So some assignment satisfies at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> clauses.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Such an assignment always exists — the average is just a value that some actual assignment cannot exceed. The same mean also gives a witness for \"at least\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — the coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> can be computed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> by repeated squaring (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>), doing each polynomial multiplication with FFT, and sum the costs.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Repeated squaring needs only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> multiplications, and each FFT multiply of degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> polynomials costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Raising to a power with a target op-count is the signal for fast exponentiation, and multiplying polynomials cheaply is exactly what FFT is for.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Squaring ladder.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is a power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> by squaring the previous result — just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> multiplications, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Cost of step <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>.</b> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th squaring works on polynomials of degree about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math>, costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> via FFT.</li>\n  <li><b>Sum it up.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, each term is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the whole computation stays within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> operations, dominated by the final squaring — matching the claim.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False — computing the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> does not require time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Stack the vectors into a matrix so the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> products become a single matrix–matrix product, then apply fast matrix multiplication.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix–vector products are one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> multiplication, which fast algorithms do in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Seeing many products of one matrix against different vectors is the cue to batch them into a single matrix product and drop below the naive cube.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Batch the products.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> have the vectors as columns. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> has columns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> products come from one matrix multiplication.</li>\n  <li><b>Use fast multiplication.</b> Fast matrix multiplication computes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (Strassen already gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.81</mn></mrow></math>), regardless of any assumption on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> — independence is never used.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because the whole batch is a single fast matrix product, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> is not needed, so the statement is false.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — for a connected regular graph, the space <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> has dimension exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Identify that space as the eigenspace of the top eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (in the normalized setting) and use connectivity to pin its multiplicity to one.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> For a connected regular graph the largest eigenvalue is simple — the all-ones vector spans it and nothing else does.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Dimension of an eigenspace\" points straight to eigenvalue multiplicity, and connectivity is exactly the property that controls the gap below the top eigenvalue.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>What the space is.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the eigenspace for eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so its dimension is the multiplicity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. (Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is the normalized adjacency matrix, whose top eigenvalue is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.)</li>\n  <li><b>The top eigenvalue is attained.</b> Each row sums to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the all-ones vector satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>; thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is an eigenvalue and the dimension is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Connectivity makes it simple.</b> It was shown in the homework that for a connected graph the second eigenvalue is strictly below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> has multiplicity exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The eigenspace is just the line through the all-ones vector, so its dimension is one.</p>\n", "2025A-p2#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> compute the dual of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> symmetric.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> rewrite <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> in the equality \"standard form\" the dual recipe applies to, read off the dual mechanically, then simplify using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is symmetric, the dual comes back looking almost exactly like the primal — same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> dualizing is just \"plug into the recipe,\" but the recipe needs a fixed template (equalities with non-negative variables), so the real work is getting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> into that template first.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Flip the inequalities.</b> Multiply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, so every constraint faces the same way.</li>\n  <li><b>Add slack to reach equalities.</b> Introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Now <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Apply the dual recipe.</b> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mi>:</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;free</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, this gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Use symmetry.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>-constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. So the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Tidy with a sign flip.</b> Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, giving the clean form <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Notice <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the mirror image of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>. Thanks to symmetry the problem is essentially self-dual — exactly what part (b) leans on.</p>\n", "2025A-p2#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is an optimal solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> show this one point is feasible for both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and its dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and gives them the same objective value; duality then forces it to be optimal.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> an equality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> satisfies both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> (primal) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> (dual) at once, so the same point lives in both problems.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> matching primal and dual objective values is the textbook certificate of optimality — no need to search over all solutions, just exhibit the pair.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, certainly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>; and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is given. Both hold.</li>\n  <li><b>Feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</b> Recall <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>: from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is dual-feasible too.</li>\n  <li><b>Same objective value.</b> The primal value at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>; the dual value at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. One point, identical value in both.</li>\n  <li><b>Conclude optimality.</b> Weak duality says every primal-feasible value is at least every dual-feasible value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Here a feasible primal point and a feasible dual point hit the <em>same</em> value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, so neither can improve past it. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> attains the primal minimum.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">In short, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> plays both roles at once and pins the two objectives together, so it must be optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — and by the same token <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025A-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Write an integer linear program for Minimum Makespan: assign <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> jobs to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> machines minimizing the heaviest machine's load.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> assignment variables, plus one extra variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> that upper-bounds every machine's load, and minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> \"Minimize the maximum\" isn't linear directly, but bounding every load by a single variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> and minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> achieves the same thing.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The standard trick for a min-max objective is to introduce an auxiliary ceiling variable and push all the maxima under it.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Assignment variables.</b> For each job <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> and machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> meaning job <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> runs on machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Makespan variable.</b> Add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>, representing the load of the last-finishing machine, which we minimize.</li>\n<li><b>The program.</b> <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>minimize</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>&</mi><mi>T</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>&</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>&</mi><mi>&</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>&</mi><mi>&</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>&</mi><mi>&</mi><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">The first constraints put each job on exactly one machine; the second say every machine's load is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. Minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> drives it down to the true maximum load, so the optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> is the minimum makespan.</p>\n", "2025A-p4#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Give a randomized poly-time algorithm that, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.99</mn></mrow></math>, outputs an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>-unbiased set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Just sample <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> at random, then show every subset-parity sum is small using Chernoff on each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> and a union bound over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> For a random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, each parity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a fair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> coin with mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so its average over many samples concentrates near <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Small deviation for every one of exponentially many events\" is the textbook Chernoff-plus-union-bound pattern, and random sampling is the natural way to build an unbiased set.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>The algorithm.</b> Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> and output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Each parity is a fair coin.</b> Fix a nonempty <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>. For uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a product of independent uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> bits, hence itself uniform on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Chernoff for one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Since the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>s</mi><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></math> for some constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Choose the sample size.</b> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> large enough that this bound drops below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Union bound over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</b> There are fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> nonempty subsets, so the chance any one fails is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, which is far below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">So with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow></math> every parity sum stays within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, meaning the random sample is already an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>-unbiased set, and drawing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> bits runs in polynomial time.</p>\n", "2025A-p5#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Give an efficient randomized algorithm deciding whether two directed graphs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> are determinantally equivalent — their symbolic adjacency determinants are equal as polynomials — with error below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Don't expand the determinants; instead test the polynomial identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by plugging in random values and comparing the two numbers.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> The difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a polynomial of degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; a random evaluation reveals whether it is the zero polynomial.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Are these two huge symbolic expressions equal\" is polynomial identity testing, and the reflex tool is Schwartz-Zippel: evaluate at a random point over a large enough set.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>The algorithm.</b> For each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, pick an independent uniform integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, substitute it in, and compute both determinants numerically (each a plain number-matrix determinant, polynomial time). Declare equivalence iff the two numbers match.</li>\n<li><b>Bound the degree.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the symbolic adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. A determinant of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix is a sum of products of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> entries, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Equivalent case.</b> If the graphs are equivalent, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so it evaluates to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for any choice of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>; the determinants always agree and the algorithm is correct with certainty.</li>\n<li><b>Non-equivalent case.</b> If not equivalent, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02262;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. By Schwartz-Zippel, a nonzero degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> polynomial evaluated over a set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vanishes with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> The only error is a false \"equivalent\", with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>, and the algorithm just computes two determinants — so it is efficient and correct enough.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025B-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False — no nonzero polynomial of degree below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> can vanish on every choice of order-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> roots of unity.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Bound the fraction of roots-of-unity inputs on which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> vanishes using Schwartz–Zippel.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Over a set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, a nonzero polynomial of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> must miss at least one point.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Nonzero polynomial vanishing on a finite set\" is exactly what Schwartz–Zippel controls, and here the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> has exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> elements, matching the degree bound.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Size the ground set.</b> The order-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> roots of unity form a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>&#x02102;</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Apply Schwartz–Zippel.</b> For a nonzero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, picking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> Since the probability is below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, some choice <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So no such polynomial vanishes on all of them, and the statement is false.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025B-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> product can be done in fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math> operations.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Cut the wide matrix into square blocks and reuse fast square multiplication on each.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Multiplying by a wide matrix is just several independent square multiplications placed side by side.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> The naive count is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>; to beat it you lean on fast square matrix multiplication (exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>), so you reshape the problem into square pieces where that tool applies.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Split into blocks.</b> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, where each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Multiply block by block.</b> Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so the whole product is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> square multiplications of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrices.</li>\n<li><b>Count the cost.</b> Each square product costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, so the total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Compare.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>, beating the naive bound.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025B-p1#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — at most half the students score at least twice the average.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Model a random student's grade and apply Markov's inequality.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Grades are non-negative, so the fraction scoring at least twice the mean is bounded by Markov.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Fraction of students above a multiple of the average\" is exactly a tail probability of a non-negative variable, which is Markov's home ground.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Set up the variable.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> be the grade of a student chosen uniformly at random. The average grade is then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and for any threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the fraction of students scoring at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Apply Markov.</b> Grades are non-negative, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Read it back.</b> That probability is just the fraction of students scoring at least twice the average, so at most half of them do.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025B-p1#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True — if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for the normalized adjacency matrix, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is bipartite.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Turn the zero determinant into an eigenvalue statement, then use the class fact linking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to bipartiteness.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> says exactly that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is an eigenvalue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A vanishing determinant means the matrix is singular, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is the standard signature of the eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — the one theorem tying the spectrum to bipartiteness.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Singular matrix gives a vector.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the matrix is singular, so there is a nonzero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Identify the eigenvalue.</b> So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is an eigenvector of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> with eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Use the class fact.</b> For a connected regular graph, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is an eigenvalue of the normalized adjacency matrix if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is bipartite, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is bipartite.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025B-p2#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> write the integer program for weighted set cover.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> one binary variable per set saying whether we take it, minimize the total cost of taken sets, and demand every element be covered by at least one.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> \"covering element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>\" means at least one chosen set contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, which is a linear \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" constraint over the sets holding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the pick/don't-pick decision on each set is naturally binary, and the coverage requirement is one inequality per element — the standard ILP shape.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Choose variables.</b> For each set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is in the cover.</li>\n<li><b>Objective.</b> Cheapest family: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Coverage constraints.</b> For each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, the chosen sets containing it must number at least one: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Integrality.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Each element constraint guarantees some chosen set covers it, so a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector is a valid cover and its objective is that cover's cost — the optimum is a minimum-weight set cover.</p>\n", "2025B-p2#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> give an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation for set cover of frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> (each element in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> sets) by relaxing and rounding the ILP from (a).</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> solve the LP relaxation, then take every set whose fractional value is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> an element's constraint sums at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> values that reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in total, so one of them must be at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> — the threshold that guarantees coverage.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the LP optimum is a lower bound on OPT, and the frequency-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> structure hands you the exact rounding threshold, so relax-and-round is the natural attack.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Relax.</b> Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, solve the LP for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Since every integer cover is LP-feasible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Round.</b> Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the cover exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>; call the result <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Feasibility.</b> Fix element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Its constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> terms; if all were below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> the sum would be below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. So some containing set has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> and is taken — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is covered.</li>\n<li><b>Cost.</b> For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math>: true if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Summing with weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>. So this runs in polynomial time and never costs more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> times optimal.</p>\n", "2025B-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> For a basic feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> with basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, if the reduced cost vector satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is optimal.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Rewrite the objective using the equality constraints so its whole dependence on the nonbasic variables is captured by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>, then compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> to any feasible point.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> After substituting out the basic variables, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>constant</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>; with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, you can only go up from the constant.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Reduced costs are literally the objective's coefficients on the free variables, so expressing the cost in terms of them turns \"is this optimal\" into a sign check.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Solve for basic variables.</b> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> invertible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>, valid at every feasible point.</li>\n<li><b>Substitute into the objective.</b> Then <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div> since the bracket is exactly the reduced cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Value at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</b> At the basic feasible solution the nonbasic variables vanish, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Any feasible point is at least as costly.</b> For any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the same identity gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the extra term is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> has the smallest objective value among all feasible points — nonnegative reduced costs mean there is nowhere cheaper to go, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is optimal.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025B-p4#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>, recover the coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Move to point-value form at the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th roots of unity: evaluate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>, divide pointwise, interpolate back.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Division of polynomials is trivial in point-value form — it's just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at each point — and the assumption <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> makes every division safe.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Evaluating and interpolating at roots of unity is what the FFT does in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so pushing the hard operation into that domain is the natural reach.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Evaluate.</b> Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. This is a DFT, done by the FFT in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Divide pointwise.</b> For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> divisions, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> total, all well-defined since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Interpolate.</b> Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. These are the values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> at the roots of unity, and interpolating them is an inverse DFT, again <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, its values at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> distinct points pin it down uniquely, so the interpolated polynomial is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. Total time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025B-p5#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Estimate the versatility <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (the fraction of legal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-colorings) to within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, in polynomial time.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Sample many random colorings, report the fraction that are legal, and use Chernoff to show the estimate concentrates.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> A uniformly random coloring is legal with probability exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the empirical legal-fraction is an unbiased estimate that sharpens as the sample grows.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Estimate an unknown fraction closely with high confidence\" is the textbook Monte-Carlo-plus-Chernoff setup, so sampling is the natural reach.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>The algorithm.</b> Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> independent uniform colorings <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math> (each vertex colored uniformly in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>). Check each for legality by scanning all edges (linear time). Output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the fraction that are legal.</li>\n<li><b>Expectation.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is legal. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> the output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> has mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — unbiased.</li>\n<li><b>Concentration.</b> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so Chernoff gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>; the deviation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> enters squared, hence the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the failure probability drops below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Sampling <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> colorings and checking each in linear time is polynomial, so we get an estimate within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025C-p1#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, then every root of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> is also a root of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> and raise the defining equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to the power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> \"Order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>\" just means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; divisibility lets you reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> by taking a power.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> When a divisibility hypothesis meets an exponent, the natural move is to factor the big exponent through the small one so the known relation kicks in.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Use the divisibility.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> for some natural number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Start from the definition.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> is a root of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Raise to the power.</b> Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> is a root of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> too. Geometrically the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> evenly spaced points sit among the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> evenly spaced ones.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p1#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> the minimum number of multiplications to multiply two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> matrices, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Build one concrete algorithm for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> matrices by treating them as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> grids of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> blocks, then count its multiplications.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> An optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> method used at the block level costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> block products, and each block product is itself a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> multiply costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> To bound a minimum from above you just exhibit any valid algorithm, and block-recursion is the natural way to turn a size-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> method into a size-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> one.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Block the matrices.</b> Given two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> matrices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, partition each into a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> grid of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> blocks (consistent since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li><b>Outer method.</b> The product respects the block layout, so multiplying the two grids uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> block-multiplications (block additions cost no scalar multiplications).</li>\n<li><b>Inner cost.</b> Each block-multiplication is a real <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> product, costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> scalar multiplications.</li>\n<li><b>Count and conclude.</b> Total scalar multiplications are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Since this is one valid method and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the best over all methods, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p1#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> True: random edge weights in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> give both a unique minimum-weight cut and a unique maximum-weight cut with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Treat cuts as sets of edges and apply the isolation lemma once for the minimum and once for the maximum, then union-bound the two failure chances.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> With weights drawn from a range <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> times the ground-set size, the isolation lemma makes a non-unique extremal set very unlikely — at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> each way.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"Unique minimum/maximum over a family of subsets under random weights\" is the exact signature of the isolation lemma, so it is the natural tool to reach for.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Model cuts as a set family.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> be the family of edge-sets that are the cuts of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>; the ground set is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, and a cut's weight is the sum of its crossing-edge weights.</li>\n<li><b>Bound the min failure.</b> Weights are uniform over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so the range is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> against ground-set size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The isolation lemma gives that the minimum-weight cut fails to be unique with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Bound the max failure.</b> Apply the same lemma to the negated weights (maximizing is minimizing the negation), so the maximum-weight cut is non-unique also with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Union bound.</b> The chance that at least one of the two bad events happens is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> So with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> both extremal cuts are unique — random weights isolate a single best and single worst cut.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p1#d": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> False: it is not the case that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is optimal for an LP if and only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Give one tiny counterexample that breaks the equivalence in both directions.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Optima can lie at non-vertex points, and vertices can be far from optimal; \"there exists an optimal vertex\" is the correct, weaker fact.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> A claimed iff is fastest to kill with a single small LP that violates each direction, rather than arguing in general.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Optimal need not be a vertex.</b> Minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Every feasible point is optimal, including all interior points of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which are not vertices.</li>\n<li><b>Vertex need not be optimal.</b> In the same box, minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>: the vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is feasible with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, while the optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So a vertex can be strictly worse than optimal.</li>\n<li><b>What is actually true.</b> For a feasible bounded LP there exists an optimal solution that is a vertex — an existence statement, not an equivalence.</li>\n<li><b>Conclude.</b> Both directions of the iff fail, so the statement is false.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p2#a": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the LP dual of the set-cover relaxation.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> write the relaxation in matrix form, push it to equality standard form with slacks, apply the mechanical dual rule, then flip signs to recognize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> once the primal is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, its dual is automatically <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> going through matrix form lets you use the one dual recipe you already know instead of guessing constraint directions.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Matrix form.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> incidence matrix, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> else <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then the constraints are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>, and the relaxation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Standard form.</b> Rewrite <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>, add slacks <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>s</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, still minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Dual rule.</b> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Flip <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</b> With the new <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the objective becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. That is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p2#b": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> the sets whose dual constraint is tight, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, form a set cover.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> suppose some element is uncovered and show you could then improve the dual, contradicting optimality of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> an uncovered element sits only in slack (non-tight) constraints, so its dual variable has room to grow &mdash; and growing it raises the objective.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> \"an optimal solution can't be improved\" is the lever, so we look for slack that would let us push a variable up.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Assume not a cover.</b> Suppose element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is in no set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math>. Then every set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math>, so its constraint is slack: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Its variable has room.</b> The dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> appears only in constraints of sets that contain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, and all of those are strict inequalities. Constraints of sets avoiding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> don't involve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> at all.</li>\n<li><b>Push it up.</b> Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> smaller than every one of those positive slacks and set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02190;</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>. All constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> still hold and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the new point is feasible.</li>\n<li><b>Contradiction.</b> The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> went up by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, beating <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> &mdash; impossible for an optimum. So no element is uncovered, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math> is a set cover.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p2#c": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> give an efficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation for set cover, where every element lies in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> sets.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> solve the dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, take the tight sets as the cover (valid by part b), then bound its cost against the LP optimum using frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> each chosen set's cost equals the sum of its elements' dual values, and when you re-sum by element, each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is counted at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> times.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> tightness turns costs into dual sums, and swapping the summation order is the natural way to expose the frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Algorithm.</b> Solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for an optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (an LP, so polynomial time) and output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> the sets whose constraint is tight. By part (b) this is a valid cover.</li>\n<li><b>Cost via tightness.</b> Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is tight, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>, and <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Swap the order.</b> Grouping by element, each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is counted once per chosen set containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>: <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><b>Use frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> sets, so each coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>, and since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> (the last step because the relaxation lower-bounds the integer optimum). So the cover costs at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> times optimal &mdash; an efficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p3#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Claim</span> after poly-many steps the lazy walk reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> write the lazy walk's transition matrix through the normalized adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, check its eigenvalues stay in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> with a spectral gap, then reuse the mixing theorem from class verbatim.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> laziness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> only rescales eigenvalues to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; this keeps the top one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and lifts the most negative one up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so nothing sits near <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> the class mixing theorem needs top eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and every other of magnitude bounded below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; an ordinary walk can have an eigenvalue near <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (bipartite-like oscillation), and the lazy step is exactly the fix.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Class facts about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>.</b> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> regular and connected, the eigenvalues of the normalized adjacency matrix satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with a gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Lazy transition matrix.</b> Staying with prob. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> and moving to a uniform neighbor with prob. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. Sharing eigenvectors with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, it has eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Top eigenvalue unchanged.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — the uniform/stationary direction survives.</li>\n  <li><b>Gap preserved.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, still bounded below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>Laziness kills the bad end.</b> From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. So every eigenvalue lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — no eigenvalue near <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to spoil convergence.</li>\n  <li><b>Conclude.</b> With top eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and all others of magnitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, the class theorem applies unchanged: after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>poly</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> steps the distribution is close to uniform, so the chance of sitting at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is roughly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, hence at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p4#x": "<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Goal</span> Give an efficient randomized test deciding whether two branching programs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> compute the same polynomial (their <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-entry), with error below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Strategy</span> Bound the degree of each polynomial, then plug random values into both and compare, using Schwartz-Zippel to control the error.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab\">Key idea</span> Two polynomials are equal iff their difference is identically zero, and a nonzero low-degree polynomial rarely vanishes at a random point.</p>\n<p class=\"ex-line\"><span class=\"ex-lab why\">Why this move</span> Equality of polynomials given as products is exactly polynomial identity testing, and Schwartz-Zippel is the go-to tool once you know a degree bound.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><b>Degree bound.</b> Each matrix entry has degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and each matrix multiplication adds at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to the degree, so after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> products the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-entries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and their difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>, have degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><b>The test.</b> Assign each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> an independent uniform value from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, compute both matrix products numerically, and answer \"equal\" iff the two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-entries match.</li>\n  <li><b>Error analysis.</b> If the programs are equal, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and the entries always match, so the test never errs. If they differ, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and by Schwartz-Zippel <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ex-line\">Each run is just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> multiplications of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> matrices, so it is fast, and a random point almost never fools a nonzero polynomial, keeping the error well under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n"};
